documents.docs/ACTIVATION_SNAPSHOTS.md:
# Copied activation snapshots

`cui_event_activation_snapshot` returns copied evidence for an activation already accepted by a submitted control. Query it after `cui_interact` accepts the activation, during ordinary frame composition. Each accepted event retains its own slot even when a frame contains multiple clicks or synthesized touch cancellation reorders the dispatch list.

```c
cui_activation_snapshot click;
if (cui_event_activation_snapshot(ui, event_index, &click)) {
    if (click.has_press && click.has_release) {
        float dx = click.release.x - click.press.x;
        (void)dx;
    }
    if (click.has_key_bounds) {
        cui_rect anchor = click.key_bounds;
        (void)anchor;
    }
}
```

The context stores a bounded snapshot beside each existing activation slot. It performs no additional per-event allocation. A pointer press is retained across frames only while the existing primary capture survives. Cancellation, touch gesture takeover, removal and disabled capture clear the retained press. Leaving the window preserves it. A release must match pointer type, contact, device and button before `has_press` becomes true. Existing routing can accept some unmatched releases; those retain release evidence with `has_press=false` instead of borrowing another contact's press.

Keyboard evidence contains the previous rendered clipped routing rectangle, even if the current composition moves the control. Accessibility activation has no fabricated press, release or keyboard bounds. Raw pointer click count zero remains zero. First-focusing-click and long-press metadata require separate authoritative host support and are not exposed here.

False leaves the output untouched. The query refuses unaccepted/cancelled events, current disabled/modal/accessibility rejection, invalid indices, null output, Core errors, inert/layout-only ancestry, measurement/prepaint and calls outside a frame. Accessibility consumption can invalidate a subsequent query; copy evidence before callbacks that may consume it. A returned value contains no pointers and survives later frames or context destruction. Frame sequence and event index identify only this context/frame, not recreated contexts or sequence wrap.

The default retains Enter-down, Space-up, focus and consumption behavior.
Core now also supports opt-in activation on key release; see
[the bounded release-key policy](validation/release-key-activation-sol6.md).
Button forwards copied evidence before callbacks; see
[Button click evidence](BUTTON_CLICK_EVIDENCE.md). Signed tab paths and
capture/bubble propagation remain separate unfinished capabilities.

Standalone Core-only headless tests cover multiple clicks, cross-frame pen capture and leave/enter, raw counts/modifiers/device, unmatched releases, cancellation, previous rendered keyboard bounds, modal/disabled/accessibility rejection, phase guards, output preservation, retained-copy lifetime and synthesized touch event reordering. Native strict C11 and ASan/UBSan/leak checks pass. Actual Zig0.16/Wasmtime49 reports136-byte snapshots and40-byte pointer samples, matching this native host; these are receipts rather than cross-platform ABI requirements. Portable full CTest148/148 passes; the final expanded fixture was rerun separately. WASI assertions remain enabled.

documents.docs/BADGE.md:
# Badge

`cui_badge_ex` attaches a count, dot or SVG icon to arbitrary child content.
Submit it every frame. The application owns the count and child actions; Badge
has no retained model. The corner marker does not change the child's measured
size or add a keyboard focus stop. A count of zero hides only the marker.

```c
#include <cui/cui_components.h>

typedef struct {
    const cui_theme *theme;
    size_t unread;
    bool opened;
} inbox_state;

static void draw_inbox(cui_context *ui, void *user) {
    inbox_state *inbox = user;
    if (cui_button(ui, cui_id_from(ui, "open-inbox"), inbox->theme,
                   cui_string("Inbox"), false))
        inbox->opened = true;
}

void draw_badged_inbox(cui_context *ui, inbox_state *inbox) {
    cui_badge_options options = cui_badge_defaults();
    options.count = inbox->unread;
    options.name = cui_string("Unread messages");
    cui_badge_ex(ui, cui_id_from(ui, "unread"), inbox->theme,
                  options, draw_inbox, inbox);
}
```

The defaults select the count variant, medium size and maximum 99. A count
above the maximum displays `99+`; setting maximum to zero deliberately displays
`0+` for positive counts. Full `size_t` counts are supported without allocation.
For a presence dot, set `variant = CUI_BADGE_DOT`. For an icon, set
`variant = CUI_BADGE_ICON` and `icon = cui_string("check")`. Icon names and paths
use the same configurable theme SVG loader as other CUI controls; applications
can supply their own assets. Set `custom_color = true` and `color` to override
the marker background, including transparent colors.

The four control sizes match the frozen source: icon diameters are 10px for
extra-small/small, 16px for medium and 24px for large; the dot is always 6px.
Count text is 10px with 2px padding and a 14px minimum width. The number's corner
offset changes with the size and displayed digit count. An icon sits at the
bottom right with a 1px border in the theme background; count and dot sit at
the top right. Ancestor clipping applies. A supplied `name` provides the status
semantic name; otherwise count text, “Notification” or “Status” is used. Name,
icon and callback data are borrowed during submission. Invalid variant, size or
nonempty null strings submit nothing and do not invoke the content callback.

The older `cui_badge` draws a standalone text pill. Use it for tags such as
“In review”; use `cui_badge_ex` for an indicator attached to content.

Headless tests cover first-frame layout, all sizes/variants, overflow text,
custom colors, icon draw commands, nonfocusable status semantics, zero-count
content preservation and invalid-input callback isolation. Arbitrary child
styles remain ordinary Core composition; there is no global text-style scope.

documents.docs/BUTTON_GROUP.md:
# ButtonGroup

ButtonGroup composes direct Button children into a joined horizontal or vertical collection. It is distinct from ToggleGroup. The Base API depends only on Core; `cui_button_group_ex` supplies styled Button paint, icons, slots, hover/focus states and inherited sizing.

The caller owns a Boolean selection array, stable nonzero child IDs, and a zero-initialized workspace. Keep these alive through submission. Allocate the bounded workspace once, rather than on a small thread stack; it supports at most 256 direct children and performs no heap allocation. On the tested 64-bit build the Base workspace is 18,016 bytes and the styled workspace is 212,584 bytes; sizes vary with ABI. Labels, icons, content callbacks and optional Button states retain their existing borrowed lifetimes. Do not reuse a workspace, submit another frame, or mutate descriptors/theme/Button states from its callbacks. Selection callbacks may update the selection array. Descriptor and theme snapshots prevent descriptor changes from affecting later events in that submission.

```c
#include <cui/cui_components.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    bool selected[3];
    cui_button_group_style_workspace *workspace;
} app;

static void select_requested(void *user, const cui_button_group_request *request) {
    app *a = user;
    memset(a->selected, 0, sizeof a->selected);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < request->selected_count; ++i)
        a->selected[request->selected_indices[i]] = true;
}

static bool compose_group(cui_context *ui, const cui_theme *theme, app *a) {
    cui_button_group_styled_item items[] = {
        {.id = 101, .label = {"Day", 3}},
        {.id = 102, .label = {"Week", 4}},
        {.id = 103, .label = {"Month", 5}},
    };
    return cui_button_group_ex(ui, 100, theme, items, a->selected, 3, a->workspace,
        (cui_button_group_style_options){
            .behavior = {.on_change = select_requested, .change_user = a},
            .override_variant = true, .variant = CUI_BUTTON_OUTLINE,
        }, NULL);
}
/* Initialize: a.workspace = calloc(1, sizeof *a.workspace);
 * Compose inside a successful begin_frame/end_frame; free at shutdown. */
```

A callback is a controlled request. Ignoring it rejects the request. Single selection requests the clicked index, including a repeated click on the selected item. Multiple selection enumerates current selected indices in ascending order, then removes the clicked selected index or appends a newly selected index. Values are reread between accepted events, so two clicks in one frame produce two ordered callbacks and the second sees the first accepted change. Final checked/value semantics and styled selection paint reflect the final model immediately. The Boolean result of standalone Button remains “any accepted activation”; its callback now runs once per accepted event, rather than collapsing queued clicks.

The default group callback policy matches the pinned source's pointer-only callback path: mouse and touch taps request selection; keyboard and accessibility activation do not invoke the group handler. `CUI_BUTTON_GROUP_ALL_ACTIVATIONS` explicitly extends this to accepted keyboard/accessibility activations. Key repeat rejection remains Core behavior. If no group handler is installed, each child's own callback remains active, including keyboard activation. With a group handler, child callbacks are replaced. A genuine collection-background pointer activation reports `item_index == SIZE_MAX` and unchanged selection; it never reuses a previous child index.

Group disabled, child disabled and child loading states combine with OR. This intentionally avoids the pinned source's builder-order-dependent disabled propagation. Loading retains its busy description and blocks activation. Cancelled input and blocked releases do not dispatch callbacks. Pointer focus, custom cursors, slots, tooltips and Button state options remain per child. Group size/variant overrides take precedence; group compact/outline flags enable those child options. The group owns its direct child boxes, orientation, zero inter-child gap and segmented shape. First/last outer corners survive, internal corners are square, and later children omit the shared left/top border. A single child preserves its own shape. There is no invented roving-focus or mandatory-selection requirement; ordinary Core Tab behavior applies.

Under explicit or ancestor inert/layout-only scope, the styled API participates in layout and permitted paint without registering semantics, dispatching selection/hover callbacks, updating tooltip state, focusing or mutating the model or caller Button state. Custom content remains an application layout callback and must avoid side effects, as with ordinary Core layout-only composition. The staged Base API instead rejects suppressed scopes.

For Base composition, open the actual group box, call `cui_base_button_group_begin`, open each stable child box and call `cui_base_button_group_item` once, then call `cui_base_button_group_end` before closing the group. Missing/duplicate registration, stale frames, cross-context workspace reuse and reentrant dispatch reject without application callbacks. Partial Core registration is not rolled back; retain ordinary Core error handling. Reset abandoned workspaces before reuse, context recreation, or a complete 64-bit frame-sequence cycle.

Core's read-only `cui_event_accepted_activation` reports only activation accepted during current widget registration, in original frame-event order. Raw `cui_event_activation` remains compatible. Current disabled/loading role/modal metadata can invalidate a record; the query does not consume it. `cui_frame_sequence` is nonzero only during ordinary interactive submission and increments on successful begin even with zero delta time. `cui_frame_is_submitting` also admits passive inert/layout-only submission. Both reject measurement, layout resolution, prepaint and outside-frame queries; neither is a clock or persistent context identity.

Evidence: focused Core and Base tests cover multiple queued events, controlled accept/reject, source/all activation policies, cancellation, background identity, disabled/loading, snapshot and workspace lifecycle. Styled tests measure joins and text containment across four legacy sizes, fourteen design sizes, two density fonts, both axes and twelve variants, plus passive ancestry. Actual Chromium 1208 runs the WASM Core/components/software painter and genuine mouse/CDP touch events, checking joined pixels, shipped SVG pixels, custom cursor and callback behavior. The SDL Xvfb fixture pushes real SDL events through the adapter and renders native draw commands; it is queued-event evidence, not physical OS/device coverage. These tests do not establish complete Button-family parity, native font coverage at every size, or physical touch-device behavior.

documents.docs/CALENDAR_PRESENTATION.md:
# Calendar presentation opt-ins

Include `cui/cui_calendar_presentation.h`. The `_ex` Calendar functions accept a separate value-only record; zero preserves the legacy draw, semantics and reduction paths. Rebuild with matching headers and library when adopting these symbols. The unpublished presentation record now includes `neighboring_days`; mixing earlier record layouts with the new library is unsupported. No binding support is implied.

```c
#include "cui/cui_calendar_presentation.h"
typedef struct {
    cui_calendar_state calendar;
    cui_date_selection selection;
} appointment_calendar;
void appointment_calendar_init(appointment_calendar *app, cui_date initial) {
    app->selection = (cui_date_selection){.start = initial};
    cui_calendar_init(&app->calendar, initial);
}
cui_calendar_result appointment_calendar_submit(cui_context *ui, const cui_theme *theme,
                                                appointment_calendar *app,
                                                cui_date host_today) {
    return cui_calendar_ex(ui, 100, theme, &app->calendar, &app->selection,
        (cui_calendar_options){.locale = cui_date_locale_builtin(CUI_DATE_LOCALE_IT)},
        (cui_calendar_presentation_options){.month_toggle = true, .today = host_today,
                                                .neighboring_days = true});
}
```

Call initialization once and submission inside each active frame. The host supplies its local civil date; the core reads no clock or timezone. Invalid/zero `today` omits decoration. Today uses theme accent and accent foreground, selected cells use the existing primary paint, and disabled cells retain muted foreground. Today never changes selection or date eligibility. Configure fonts for the labels and consume draw/semantic data before the next frame.

With `month_toggle=true` and one displayed month (`months` zero or one), separate localized month and year headers open their respective grids. Activating an open header closes it; Escape returns to days and focuses that grid's header. Selecting a month returns to days and focuses its first enabled date. Year selection retains the legacy transition through the month grid. Header semantics expose selected/expanded state. The month header ID is `cui_calendar_month_toggle_id(root)`; the year header retains navigation part 5. Multimonth calendars ignore the month-toggle option. Existing arrows, Home/End, PageUp/Down, Enter/Space and policy checks remain in the same reducer; this adds no global key listener.

`cui_calendar_reduce_ex` and `cui_calendar_submit_ex` retain the split reducer/submission contract; Base performs both and Styled also paints. Locale strings and disabled predicate/user resources are borrowed and must remain valid through use and frame consumption. The presentation record contains no retained pointer. Predicates must not mutate or recursively submit active UI. Omission of the widget is allowed after its outer call and frame consumption; persistent application state remains caller-owned.

Navigation reports `navigated`, never `changed` or `activated` by itself. Existing typed CHANGE wrappers are for legacy Calendar options; there is no extended observer wrapper or event-facade bridge in this slice. Use the returned aggregate result for this API. Existing callback ordering is unchanged.

`neighboring_days=true` paints and submits valid outside-month cells as selectable dates. Muted unselected neighbors retain ordinary eligibility checks; selected/range, disabled and today paint use the precedence above. Neighbor occurrences have date-and-panel IDs; in-month cells retain `cui_calendar_day_id`. Only the canonical cursor is a sequential day tab stop. A neighbor can still receive pointer/programmatic/AT focus and Enter/Space activation. Selection focuses the canonical occurrence, retaining the displayed window when that date is in another visible panel or rebasing to its month otherwise. Existing CUI range endpoint swapping is preserved; frozen GPUI instead restarts an earlier second endpoint and recenters on range start. This slice does not change that established CUI selection model.

`cui_calendar_cell_resolve(root, first_month, first_weekday, panel, cell, &info)` returns a copied date, ID and neighboring flag for Base layout, automation or host inspection. A valid first month is normalized to day 1; weekday is modulo seven. Panel must be 0..2 and cell 0..41. It does not test whether the panel was submitted or the date is enabled. Invalid inputs, unrepresentable year-edge cells and zero-derived occurrence/canonical IDs return false and zero the output; NULL output returns false. Keep ordinary root-scoped ID collision discipline: no guarantee is made across unrelated caller roots. Repeated visible dates intentionally have distinct neighbor IDs. Queued input resolves against the outer call's original displayed grid and applies current policy per activation; preceding navigation cannot reinterpret the same ID as a different civil date.

This implements independent month access, host-supplied today paint and opt-in neighboring cells under CUI's one-to-three-month/six-row bounds. Arbitrary item styling and unrestricted month counts remain outside this API. Seven builtin locale tables and custom tables already exist; this is not an OS locale engine, arbitrary date-pattern parser, non-Gregorian calendar or RTL layout implementation. Frozen GPUI source does not define a dedicated Calendar arrow-key contract; CUI's existing traversal contract is preserved.

Legacy DatePicker keeps zero presentation options; the additive [DatePicker Calendar API](DATE_PICKER_CALENDAR.md) forwards this record through its existing popup composition. The cell helper and presentation calls do not allocate or retain per-cell pointers; frame data and borrowed locale/policy lifetimes remain as described above.

The legacy range, year-view and recenter defaults above can be selected independently through [Calendar behavior policies](CALENDAR_BEHAVIOR.md), including optional completed-endpoint validation. These are separate value-only options and APIs; they do not resize the presentation record or change legacy calls.

documents.docs/CHART_RADAR_TOOLTIP_TITLE.md:
# Radar custom-label tooltip title

Opt into `CUI_CHART_RADAR_TOOLTIP_TITLE_SOURCE_LABEL` through the appended `cui_chart_options.radar_tooltip_title_mode` field. This follows the pinned GPUI component 0.7.1 `RadarLabel::Element` rule: paired custom dimension label measure/paint callbacks supply no automatic tooltip title. The series rows and hover marks still render. Plain text labels retain their datum-label fallback title.

```c
options.kind = CUI_CHART_RADAR;
options.radar_tooltip_title_mode = CUI_CHART_RADAR_TOOLTIP_TITLE_SOURCE_LABEL;
options.radar_label_measure = measure_dimension;
options.radar_label_paint = paint_dimension;
```

Zero is `CUI_CHART_RADAR_TOOLTIP_TITLE_LEGACY` and preserves the existing fallback. The source policy affects only Radar and only when both custom label callbacks are set. An explicit `tooltip_title` callback overrides the fallback, including an empty result that suppresses visible title text under the existing CUI callback contract. Legacy `tooltip_content` retains complete precedence and skips automatic title/row formatter construction. `CURRENT_FRAME` continues to reject legacy layout/measure tooltip callbacks; this option does not permit layout during prepaint.

The component resolves the same fallback in legacy composition and current-frame deferred tooltip painting, including retained exit fade. Strings and callback users follow existing caller-owned lifetimes through the frame; no new title data is retained. Invalid enum values and a nonzero policy on another Chart kind are rejected before styled UI/state mutation or Base raster mutation. The option changes tooltip metadata, not raster geometry, and does not add cache-key terms.

This is a bounded title fix. It does not provide typed arbitrary tooltip rows, reproduce popover Gaussian shadows, add a keyboard/legend contract, or certify native accessibility announcements. Source `Some(empty_title)` layout presence versus CUI's existing empty string suppression remains an independent typed-content contract.

The native test covers legacy/current-frame paths, paired/partial/plain labels, explicit and empty overrides, legacy custom-content precedence/exact callback counts, exit retention/settling, copied options/state, invalid atomicity, and unchanged raster-cache keys. It runs through CTest and the portable WASI runner. The standalone browser check uses Zig 0.16.0 WASM, current-frame immutable pointer snapshots, and actual Chromium canvas text/raster pixels for stationary hover across legacy/source/explicit title toggles; it also runs in the website deployment workflow.

documents.docs/CHART_TOOLTIP_APPEARANCE.md:
# Opt-in Chart tooltip appearance

`cui_chart_options.tooltip_style` applies explicit typography, spacing, colors and boxed/bare placement to automatic and copied typed tooltip content. A NULL pointer preserves existing tooltip behavior, including the old border. Automatic opt-in content uses the same bounded copying row arena as typed content: 32 rows, 4096 aggregate bytes, 1024 bytes per string, valid UTF-8. The style is borrowed only during synchronous composition and copied by value into current-frame prepaint; the callback rebinds to its copied payload, so caller stack storage can disappear after `cui_chart` returns. Caller data, callback users and existing raster buffers retain their existing borrowed lifetimes.

```c
cui_theme theme = cui_theme_light();
cui_chart_tooltip_style tooltip;
float rem_pixels = 16; /* host's current rem projection */
/* GPUI's default inherited relative line height, resolved for compact text.
 * Supply the actual resolved value when the host overrides line height. */
float inherited_line_pixels = roundf(.75f * rem_pixels * 1.618034f);
if (cui_chart_tooltip_style_project(&theme, rem_pixels,
                                  inherited_line_pixels, &tooltip)) {
    options.tooltip_style = &tooltip;
    options.interaction_mode = CUI_PLOT_INTERACTION_CURRENT_FRAME;
    cui_chart(ui, chart_id, &state, &options, cui_string("Revenue"));
}
```

The checked projection sets compact font .75rem, vertical gap .25rem, label/value gap .75rem, swatch gap .375rem, swatch size and padding .5rem. Minimum width stays 150px and Chart cursor gap 8px. Background/foreground use Theme.popover/popover_foreground; CUI Theme.muted supplies the muted foreground role. Surface radius uses Theme.radius; swatch radius is half. The sigma-zero ring shadow uses Theme.text (source foreground) RGB with alpha .1, quantized to 26/255; source projection replaces its inherited alpha. It is distinct from popover foreground. Transparent colors are intentional values. Font and line height must be positive; other dimensions may be zero. Enum and finite bounded dimensions, including minimum viable spacing, are validated before interaction/state mutation. Factory failure leaves output untouched.

The source's `text_xs` sets font size without changing inherited line height. Pinned gpui-pre 0.3.8 defaults to a font-relative 1.618034 line height and rounds its resolved pixel value; Component Root sets rem size to Theme.font_size. The factory takes a resolved inherited line height explicitly instead of assuming glyph height equals font size. Measured glyph widths determine fitting; inherited line boxes determine vertical rhythm, with measured glyphs centered inside those boxes. A row reserves max(line height,swatch size) when any row has a swatch. Plain rows among swatched rows keep the empty slot; transparent swatches also keep it. Gaps appear only between existing title/rows, including an empty-present title. Values share the right edge. Existing per-value color overrides remain authoritative.

BOXED (zero enum) uses the projected surface and midpoint cursor flips independently on both axes. BARE uses the full plot extent at its origin, removes padding/minimum/surface/cursor anchoring, stretches row value alignment to the plot edge, and preserves the tooltip's deferred fade. Crosshairs and marks stay local. BARE currently supports bounded single-line structured content: intrinsic width exceeding the plot width fails rather than inventing wrapping. Source arbitrary element wrapping remains a separate gap.

Explicit CURRENT_FRAME follows immutable current bounds and pointer visibility under stationary scroll/resize. A style alone does not change AUTO's existing manual-pointer contract. Explicit LEGACY continues to use retained origin/manual pointer information; it does not gain current-frame geometry. Legacy custom `tooltip_content`/`tooltip_measure` remains supported, receives the projected content box, and must apply the host child font/line height itself. Custom measured sizes must be finite/nonnegative/bounded. Without a custom measurement the legacy content height remains a bounded 48px fallback. Arbitrary layout callbacks are still rejected by CURRENT_FRAME.

Invalid style returns false before output or owner mutation. Legacy copied structured content and custom measurement are staged before layout/semantics. Oversized measured content fails without publishing owner state. Current-frame measurement/emission failure rolls back that owner's paint output and state, reporting Core layout error; layout clip commands can remain. Host callback/measurement side effects, motion frame requests, borrowed raster writes and later global end-frame failures remain outside the existing transaction scope. Clear a reported Core error before retrying corrected input.

Source popovers have an outside translucent foreground ring and two Gaussian shadow layers. The consumed `ring_color` paints a filled rounded shadow before the surface in both boxed paths: bounds expanded 1px, ORIGINAL radius, sigma 0. Spread changes bounds, not corner radii, matching pinned Window.paint_drop_shadows. The source shadow also fills beneath the surface; transparent background or hover progress exposes that contribution. Legacy emits a separate bounded portal before the surface; current-frame emits the equivalent deferred filled rectangle, without Core input/layout mutation. BARE emits neither ring nor surface. Headless tests prove geometry, order, role distinction and alpha through ancestor opacity; the WASM probe proves outside/inside pixel compositing, including clipped half-opacity owners, translucent surfaces and controlled hover progress. A corner probe distinguishes the original radius from the former radius+1 geometry. This establishes source shadow geometry/compositing behavior, not exact native GPUI shader/antialias pixel identity. Gaussian sigma 3/2 falloff remains a rendering gap; there are no shadow fields or approximations. Arbitrary current-frame interactive layout, source wrapping and accepted mobile long-press delivery/prevention also remain incomplete. This API does not establish full Chart or native accessibility parity.

`tests/unit/test_chart_tooltip_appearance.c` checks source projection at three rem sizes, inherited line boxes independent of glyph height, all seven automatic Chart kinds, both consumers and appearances, empty-present titles, mixed/transparent/plain slots, larger swatches/zero gaps, placement flips, clipping/opacity, stationary current bounds, copied storage/relocated owners, malformed styles/oversized content rollback, ring geometry/order/theme alpha, custom legacy measurement and finite exit. `tests/web/test_chart_tooltip_appearance.py` compiles a standalone pinned Zig 0.16 WASM probe and checks actual Chromium canvas pixels for all eight automatic/typed × boxed/bare × consumer combinations plus stationary owner move/resize and outside/inside ring pixels with clipping/opacity. Its custom proof renderer does not certify all adapter styling or native platform behavior.

Worker validation: strict warning build and scoped native test passed; GCC ASAN/UBSAN/leaks with runtime use-after-return detection passed; real Chromium 1208 WASM probe passed. Full CTest on isolated f8b7f43 completed 125/126, only stale generated distribution failed. Integrator registers the new native/browser tests and regenerates distributions; worker changes no global registrations.

A focused correction replaces the former outline/border approximation: the pre-fix native regression against093e927 fails because the source requires filled-under-surface shadow geometry with unchanged radii. Full baseline CTest still reports125/126, only stale distribution. Gaussian layers are separate remaining work.

In the website Chart preview, choose Classic tooltip, Popover tooltip or Bare tooltip. Combine the choice with Custom tooltip rows to compare automatic and callback content. The gallery resolves rem from the current theme font size and supplies the inherited compact line height; other applications can supply their own resolved metrics.

documents.docs/CHART_TYPED_TOOLTIP.md:
# Copied Chart tooltip content

Append `tooltip_build` and `tooltip_build_user` to opt into structured tooltip content. The builder copies title, label, and value bytes synchronously; local formatting buffers can be changed or leave scope immediately after a setter returns. No builder or string pointers escape into owner state.

```c
#include "cui/cui_chart.h"
#include <stdio.h>

static bool tooltip(const cui_chart_tooltip_info *info,
                    cui_chart_tooltip_builder *builder, void *user) {
    (void)user;
    char value[48];
    int written = snprintf(value, sizeof value, "%.0f", info->values[0]);
    if (written < 0 || (size_t)written >= sizeof value)
        return false;
    if (info->has_fallback_title &&
        !cui_chart_tooltip_set_title(builder, info->fallback_title))
        return false;
    cui_chart_tooltip_row row = {
        .has_swatch = true, .swatch = info->series_colors[0],
        .label = info->series_labels[0],
        .value = {value, (size_t)written},
    };
    if (!cui_chart_tooltip_add_row(builder, &row))
        return false;
    return cui_chart_tooltip_add_row(builder, &(cui_chart_tooltip_row){
        .label = {"Total", 5}, .value = {value, (size_t)written},
    });
}
/* options.tooltip_build = tooltip; */
```

Choose **Custom tooltip rows** in the Chart gallery to see a swatched series row
and a plain reporting-period row. The application callback receives real values
and fills its rows with these copying helpers. Configure `options.tooltip_build`
before submitting the Chart; retain any `tooltip_build_user` data through frame
completion. This callback describes paint content and should not perform
application actions or change the UI during prepaint.

The immutable callback info contains Chart kind, retained visual datum/index, at most eight raw values (four Open/High/Low/Close values for candles), default series labels/colors, and optional fallback title. Sankey supplies current retained throughput. Pie supplies raw values rather than formatted share percentages; callers wanting ratios have their caller-owned data/user context. Automatic title/value/label/value-color/pie formatter callbacks are skipped when the builder is selected. The builder supplies the complete title/row content. `tooltip_content` or `tooltip_measure` together with a builder is rejected before mutation; existing custom layout callbacks retain their original behavior when no builder is supplied.

`AUTO` plus the new builder selects current-frame pointer/snapshot painting. Existing zero/no-builder AUTO behavior stays unchanged. Explicit `LEGACY` preserves the legacy manual/event pointer path and stages a local next-state, the copied builder, and the shared measured row layout before layout/semantic output. Bounds tracking targets the persistent original owner state. Current-frame builds once during prepaint and uses immutable current bounds, hover/occlusion state, and fade progress. Legacy builds once during composition. Both rebuild while retained exit content is visible and stop when the hover channel settles.

A row's `has_swatch` is presence, including an alpha-zero color. If any row has a swatch, all labels reserve the same 14-unit slot (8 swatch plus 6 gap); a plain row leaves that slot empty. With only plain rows, labels start at the tooltip padding. Values align to one shared right edge; `has_value_color` selects an explicit color, including transparent, otherwise foreground. Calling `set_title` with an empty string creates a present title row and retains its space. Without a setter, there is no title row. An empty builder renders the existing empty padded box.

Limits are 32 rows, 4096 aggregate copied bytes, and 1024 bytes per string. Replacing a title still consumes copied byte capacity. Strings must be valid UTF-8; NULL data is allowed only for length zero. NULL rows, malformed UTF-8, over-capacity strings/rows, or a failed setter/add-row latch a failure. Ignoring a returned false cannot publish partial content. Callback false also rejects the whole builder.

Legacy builder failure leaves caller state and component layout/semantic output unchanged. A failed current-frame callback rolls back the complete owner paint output and does not commit next-state. Core reports a current-frame callback failure as `CUI_ERROR_LAYOUT`; clear the reported error before retrying corrected input. Writer command-budget and copied-text limits remain bounded. Legacy commits after successful synchronous emissions. Frame requests already generated while stepping appearance/hover, borrowed pixel-buffer writes, callback/measurement host side effects, and later global `end_frame` capacity/layout failures are outside the component's transactional scope; they cannot be restored by this API. Data, callbacks, and user remain caller-owned and must be live through composition/prepaint and renderer completion as required by existing Chart contracts. Callback info/builder are synchronous-only; callbacks must not retain them or perform layout/input/semantic work during prepaint.

This slice uses the existing tooltip box styling: font 11, radius 4, minimum width 150, padding/cursor gap 8, row height 16, title step 18, label/value gap 12, background/foreground/grid border, and muted labels at .7 foreground alpha. It adds no speculative theme/style API and does not claim source popover style parity. Pinned 0.7.1 source uses popover colors/radius, compact rem typography, optional structured/freeform content, unboxed appearance, and an outside ring with Gaussian shadows; those contracts remain separate. Arbitrary custom layout/content in current-frame painting and accepted mobile long-press phase/prevention also remain gaps. This is no full Chart-family or native accessibility certification.

Native coverage checks raw callback values/labels/colors for all seven Chart kinds, copied overwritten stack buffers, plain/mixed/transparent slots, value alignment/color, title-only/empty-present/empty content, exact row/byte limits, invalid UTF-8 and latched failures, formatter precedence, both consumers, owner relocation and persistent bounds, unknown pointer, deferred clipping/ancestor opacity/modal exclusion, and finite retained exit. The standalone Zig 0.16.0 WASM test checks actual Chromium canvas text/raster pixels for both consumers, copied rows, failed-owner rollback, and stationary pointer under controlled owner move/resize. It is a custom proof renderer, not adapter-wide styling certification. GCC ASAN/UBSAN/leaks with the runtime use-after-return option passed; a separate Clang always-use-after-return attempt was unavailable because no Clang executable was installed. The headless test runs through CTest and the portable WASI runner; the standalone pixel check also runs in the website deployment workflow.

documents.docs/COMBOBOX_EVENTS.md:
# Combobox selection notifications

`cui_base_combobox_events` and `cui_combobox_events` add synchronous selection CHANGE observers to single-selection Combobox. Legacy APIs, query editing, Select, List/Menu and multi-selection retain their behavior. Shared pointer-free payloads come from [selection events](SELECTION_EVENTS.md); this does not enable bridge typed-selection tags or uniform family coverage.

```c
#include <cui/cui_choice.h>

static void selected_changed(void *user, const cui_selection_change *change) {
    cui_id *last_seen = user;
    *last_seen = change->value;
}

/* During active composition; query, scratch, selection and popup belong to app. */
static cui_combobox_event_result search(cui_context *ui, const cui_theme *theme,
    cui_edit *query, const cui_choice_item *items, size_t count,
    cui_choice_item *scratch, size_t capacity, cui_id *selected,
    cui_choice_popup *popup, cui_id *last_seen) {
    return cui_combobox_events(ui, 2, theme, cui_string("Find account"), query,
        items, count, scratch, capacity, selected, popup,
        (cui_choice_popup_options){.viewport = {0, 0, 640, 480}},
        (cui_selection_handler){selected_changed, last_seen});
}
```

Initialize popup once with `cui_choice_popup_init` and a stable nonzero popup ID. The Base entry point matches `cui_base_combobox` arguments followed by a handler; the Styled entry point likewise appends a handler to `cui_combobox`. Base still draws nothing and handles query/Enter reduction; Styled adds query/option rendering and popup-list selection. The caller supplies a single-line editor and filtered scratch capacity at least equal to item count. Existing matching is ASCII case-insensitive substring search, byte-exact otherwise. Committed IDs remain stable under filtering and are not replaced by array indices.

`cui_combobox_event_result.legacy` preserves the complete old `changed/query_changed/scrolled/matching_count/error` result. `.events` contains preflight `valid`, active `submitted`, actual observer invocation `change_count`, and `count_overflow`. Counts saturate at `SIZE_MAX` while dispatch continues. Missing observer preserves ordinary editing/selection and reports zero callbacks. Disabled controls can submit but emit no selection notifications; inert/layout-only ancestry emits none. Scratch/argument errors remain explicit and do not report observer calls; a valid preflight is not a promise against later editing/render errors.

The callback reports an applied selection transition, not an owner-rejectable request. `cui_selection_change` copies actual `previous/value`, attempted `item`, common metadata and ACTIVATION reason. Here ACTIVATION describes semantic option confirmation, including Enter. Metadata kind is **CHANGE**, not ACTIVATE, and the reason does not imply a Core activation snapshot. Query-only edits, cursor navigation, external owner assignments, unmatched Enter, and confirming the existing value emit no selection CHANGE. Query edits have their own `.legacy.query_changed`; no query/SUBMIT event is inferred from selection.

Base first reduces query editing/filtering and Enter confirmation; Styled then reduces popup-list option input if still open. Observers run synchronously at the existing selection-write points, without sorting, replay or deferred delivery. Event indices can decrease across those phases: a later-index Enter transition can precede an earlier-index captured pointer release. Use indices as truthful input identity, not a promise of globally sorted callback delivery. Preserve existing text-before-Enter versus Enter-before-text behavior and the complete list pass before popup close. Legacy `.changed` may collapse/overwrite transitions in its existing paths; it is not a callback count or proof of final owner acceptance.

An observer may update the external selection; later events and final painting/results see that owner state according to the existing pointer-owned contract. Such writes do not recursively synthesize another notification. Each payload is frozen before the observer runs. Consuming later input changes later eligibility; no prequeued callback batch overrides consumption. Do not repeat the same side effect from both the callback and aggregate result.

`event.target` is the public input ID. Enter's direct key path copies real KEYBOARD/frame/index metadata with **no HAS_ACTIVATION**. The option-list seam copies a genuine accepted snapshot only if Core already makes it available, retaining the actual child `activation.target`; it normally registers option semantics after reduction, so snapshots are absent there too. The fallback records only source proven by the actual event type plus its frame/index. No pointer coordinates, touch snapshot or accessibility activation evidence is fabricated from source alone. See [metadata presence/lifetime conventions](COMPONENT_EVENTS.md).

Current Core excludes OPTION from accessibility ACTIVATE support. FOCUS is supported, but focus alone does not synchronize the retained list cursor. This slice preserves those limits and tests explicit focus/navigation rather than claiming successful accessibility option activation. Enabling that capability needs a separate Core review.

Handler/user/payload are borrowed through synchronous submission. Pointer-free payload copies survive later frames and context destruction. Keep active item arrays, editor/scratch/popup/selection, callback users and render resources alive through their existing boundaries. Copied handler/options changes affect later calls. Schedule removal/teardown for after submission/frame completion: active context destruction, freeing active state and recursive same-frame submission remain unsupported. No runtime destruction guard, queue, scheduler or request binding is introduced.

`tests/unit/test_combobox_events.c` exercises real Base/Styled query and Enter paths, both text/Enter orders, filtered-out committed IDs, same-value confirmation, missing handler, scratch errors, disabled/inert/omitted calls, real pointer/touch input, owner writes/later consumption, existing accessibility boundaries, phase-order evidence and copied lifetime. The mixed-phase case uses a genuine cross-frame option pointer capture, restores input focus and the caller-owned active cursor, then queues release/Enter/text; it checks callback indices1 then0 without fabricated snapshots. Legacy choice and Select suites remain compatibility checks. Run native tests only through the invisible wrapper:

```sh
python scripts/test_local.py --build-dir build -- -R '^(combobox_events|select_events|choice)$'
```

[Private plumbing checkpoint](design/COMBOBOX_EVENTS_SLICE.md) records the implementation boundary. Controlled selection rejection, query/edit events, confirmation/lifecycle events, multi-selection, foreign request hooks and parity promotion remain deferred.

documents.docs/COMPONENT_AUTHORING.md:
# Authoring a custom design system

Call base behavior inside your own Clay-backed box, then draw with your tokens. Base functions do not import colors, fonts or styled components.

```c
cui_id id = cui_id_from(ui, "notify");
cui_begin_box(ui, id, (cui_box){.width=240, .height=40, .padding=8});
bool changed = cui_base_switch(ui, id, cui_string("Notifications"),
                               &settings.notifications, false);
cui_text(ui, cui_string(settings.notifications ? "Enabled" : "Disabled"),
         16, my_foreground);
cui_end_box(ui);
```

The box and semantic behavior use the same ID. Use a different scoped key for each child. Domain values belong to the caller; base functions request changes synchronously through controlled values and return change status. Draw from updated state and inspect `cui_interaction` for focus, hover and press. Current styled controls deliberately reuse this seam. `examples/unstyled` compiles independently of theme/style APIs, and the terminal theme uses the same behavior with square corners and amber/ink tokens.

Compound components must use explicit behavioral models and focus scopes. Do not duplicate keyboard rules in their painters. Add behavioral tests, semantic assertions, a real story and limitation docs before raising manifest status. Full verified status requires every acceptance criterion in initial-prompt.md.

documents.docs/CONTENT_STYLING.md:
# Semantic alerts, bubbles and table rows

Alerts select Info, Success, Warning or Error using `cui_alert_options.kind`.
The theme provides independent `info`, `success`, `warning` and `danger` colors;
the border and default SVG icon follow that color. Set `icon_name` to another
named SVG or supply the legacy `icon` text override. The gallery demonstrates
all four kinds, with a controlled, dismissible success alert.

Composable bubbles support `CUI_BUBBLE_INFO`, `CUI_BUBBLE_SUCCESS`,
`CUI_BUBBLE_WARNING` and `CUI_BUBBLE_ERROR`. Each uses a tinted theme color.
Compose arbitrary children between `cui_begin_bubble` and `cui_end_bubble`.

Static Table rows can provide their own background independently of header
semantics. This example alternates application-owned row colors:

```c
cui_table_begin(ui, table_id, &theme, 480);
for (unsigned row = 0; row < 4; row++) {
    cui_table_begin_row_ex(ui, table_id + row + 1, &theme,
        (cui_table_row_options){
            .height = 40, .header = row == 0, .custom_background = true,
            .background = row == 0 ? theme.accent
                : row % 2 ? theme.surface : theme.background});
    /* Compose your cells with cui_table_begin_cell / cui_table_end_cell. */
    cui_table_end_row(ui);
}
cui_table_end(ui);
```

Choose foreground colors appropriate to a custom background. The existing
`cui_table_begin_row` retains its previous default behavior. A DataTable has its
own options and row callback contract; this API applies to the composable Table.
The headless tests verify distinct semantic colors and actual row drawing in
both light and dark themes. These options do not imply complete component parity.

documents.docs/CONTROL_EVENTS.md:
# Button and Check event facades

`cui_control_events.h` and the Styled declarations in `cui_components.h` add typed synchronous event facades for Button and Checkbox/Check. Existing entry points keep their behavior. [Switch/Toggle facades](BOOLEAN_EVENTS.md) are also implemented; group controls and language request bindings remain deferred; this is not uniform coverage of all 78 families. See the [design checkpoint](design/COMPONENT_EVENTS_CONTRACT.md), [coverage inventory](design/COMPONENT_EVENTS_COVERAGE.md) and [binding limitations](design/COMPONENT_EVENTS_BINDINGS.md).

## Controlled Checkbox requests

The value is an immutable entry snapshot. The application accepts by assigning its own value, or rejects by leaving it unchanged. Subsequent events, painting and semantics in this invocation still use the entry snapshot; the next submission observes accepted owner state. No notification of an external assignment is synthesized.

```c
#include <cui/cui_components.h>

typedef struct {
    cui_check_state checked;
    bool allow_changes;
} account_state;

static void request_check(void *user, const cui_component_check_request *request) {
    account_state *account = user;
    if (account->allow_changes)
        account->checked = request->requested;
}

static cui_component_action_result account_checkbox(cui_context *ui, cui_id id,
                                                     const cui_theme *theme,
                                                     account_state *account) {
    return cui_checkbox_events(ui, id, theme, cui_string("Receive updates"),
        account->checked, (cui_checkbox_options){0},
        (cui_component_check_handler){request_check, account});
}
```

`cui_component_check_request` contains typed `current` and `requested` states plus `event` metadata with kind `REQUEST`. Unchecked or mixed requests checked; checked requests unchecked. Every accepted activation produces a separate request from the same entry value. Two activations in one submission therefore propose the same next value, even if the first is accepted. Missing handler is read-only: no value write and zero requests, although accepted activation attempts are still counted.

`cui_base_check_events(ui, id, name, value, options, handler)` supplies drawing-free behavior inside a caller box with the same ID. Base preserves `cui_check_options.no_pointer_focus`; Styled `cui_checkbox_events` selects source Checkbox behavior, preserving existing focus on pointer activation. Keyboard tab/focus policies, role overrides, required/invalid metadata, indicator/style slots and motion remain the existing Checkbox policies. Omitted options default as before except that these new by-value entry points are always controlled. Old `on_change` and `change_user` must both be NULL; conflicting handler configuration fails preflight rather than being ignored or dispatched twice.

## Button activation

`cui_button_events(ui, id, theme, name, options, handler)` uses `cui_component_activation_handler` with a callback `(void *user, const cui_component_event_metadata *event)`. Its event kind is `ACTIVATE`, not value change. A missing new handler still reports accepted activation counts.

Legacy options remain usable for migration. Existing hover processing precedes click dispatch. Per accepted event the order is legacy `on_click`, old `on_click_event`, then the new handler, followed by existing paint/tooltip work after iteration. Existing click evidence is frozen before the legacy callback; a callback consuming accessibility input does not erase evidence delivered to the new handler. Original `options.action_user`, including hover, stays separate from the new handler's user. Prefer one callback or frame-result path for each side effect; performing the same action from both dispatch and result duplicates it.

## Results and lifecycle

`cui_component_action_result` contains:

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `valid` | Arguments/options passed preflight; later Core/paint errors remain possible. |
| `submitted` | Ordinary active submission reached, including disabled controls; false outside submission or in inert/layout-only ancestry. Passive visuals may still compose. |
| `activation_count` | Accepted activations observed by this invocation. |
| `request_count` | Check request callback invocations; zero for Button. |
| `count_overflow` | A count saturated at `SIZE_MAX`; dispatch continues in event order. |

Results aggregate one invocation; copying/reading a result never dispatches or mutates a value. Counts do not report owner acceptance. Disabled/inert controls emit no user callbacks. Button loading also suppresses activation; Checkbox gains no loading state. Missing/omitted immediate calls emit nothing. No COMMIT, SUBMIT, CHANGE, propagation control or default-prevention promise is added to these discrete APIs.

Payload/handler/user are borrowed through synchronous submission only. Metadata and Check request structs are pointer-free and copyable by value, including beyond frame/context lifetime. Appearance strings, icon resources and caller state retain their existing boundaries; some paint resources must live through renderer completion. [Shared metadata](COMPONENT_EVENTS.md) explains real input evidence, absent fields, context-local identities and safe copying of other borrowed UTF-8 payloads. Copying an arbitrary `cui_event` is not a text deep copy.

Callbacks may update unrelated application state and the external value owner. Changing a caller descriptor affects a later invocation, not the copied descriptor in flight. Consuming later input can change its later eligibility; callbacks are not prequeued. Request view removal or application shutdown in application state, then perform removal/freeing/teardown after the active submission and frame finish. Do not recursively submit the same component/frame, destroy the active context, or free active users/state/resources during dispatch. **There is no runtime context-destruction guard** in this slice. Bridge runner deferred destruction does not imply such a Core guarantee.

The header's `cui_control_events_private_*` identifiers are forwarding implementation details, not application entry points. No allocation, registration, worker, scheduler, Core event routing or backend changes are introduced. New source APIs are not automatically exposed by existing SDK/binding event queues. The integrator owns module registration, generated distributions/API indexes and consumer human/LLM resources.

The focused `tests/unit/test_control_events.c` covers per-event legacy/new order and users, pointer/touch/key/accessibility evidence, rejected and accepted owners, immutable multi-request snapshots, next-submission semantics, read-only/missing handlers, Base versus Styled focus, disabled/loading/passive suppression, invalid handler conflicts, outside-frame calls and saturation bookkeeping. Existing Button and Checkbox suites remain compatibility checks. Run registered tests without desktop windows:

```sh
python scripts/test_local.py --build-dir build -- -R 'control_events|button_click|button_callback|checkbox_source'
```

## Run a complete native example

The [settings example](../examples/control_events/README.md) builds a real SDL window with an owner-controlled Checkbox, an Apply button and deferred panel removal. It demonstrates acceptance and rejection, passive result inspection without duplicate side effects, and the next-submission snapshot boundary. Its README includes exact dependency, compiler, font, build and invisible-test commands. The separate [async fetch example](../examples/async_fetch/README.md) demonstrates returning promptly from a synchronous handler and applying worker completions on the UI thread.

documents.docs/CORE_PREPAINT.md:
# Paint after layout

Use `cui_set_prepaint` when drawing depends on a box's geometry in the frame being
rendered. Examples include a chart crosshair, a clipped selection overlay or a
fixed paint-only tooltip. Normal component construction still determines layout
and accessibility before this callback runs.

Register on an open box with a nonzero ID. CUI copies the payload into bounded
frame storage and invokes the callback after its single layout pass, before the
renderer consumes the finished draw list.

```c
#include <cui/cui.h>

typedef struct {
    cui_color normal, hovered;
} highlight_colors;

static bool paint_highlight(const cui_prepaint_info *info, const void *data,
                            cui_prepaint_writer *writer) {
    const highlight_colors *colors = data;
    cui_draw_command command = {
        .type = CUI_DRAW_RECT,
        .bounds = info->bounds,
        .color = info->pointer_visible ? colors->hovered : colors->normal,
    };
    return cui_prepaint_emit(writer, &command, false);
}

bool highlight_panel(cui_context *ui, cui_id id) {
    highlight_colors colors = {{24, 30, 40, 255}, {34, 55, 78, 255}};
    cui_begin_box(ui, id, (cui_box){.width = 320, .height = 120});
    bool registered = cui_set_prepaint(ui, (cui_prepaint_options){
        .callback = paint_highlight,
        .payload = &colors,
        .payload_bytes = sizeof colors,
        .command_budget = 3,
        .hover_hitbox = true,
    });
    cui_end_box(ui);
    return registered;
}
```

The three-command budget covers the rectangle and its two clipping commands.
The local paint stream uses global coordinates and clips to the box's current
visible bounds. It appears above the box's underlay and below its children.
Use `info->bounds` to position paint and `info->visible_bounds` to inspect clipping.
`pointer_visible` includes current geometry, passive overlay occlusion and modal
permission. A pointer position is unknown until a host supplies one.

For a floating paint-only tooltip, pass `true` to `cui_prepaint_emit`. That deferred
stream clips to the viewport and uses the requested layer; zero inherits the
owner's layer. Its clip commands also count toward the budget. A layer cannot
precede its owner, and modal rules can deny deferred output. Use
`cui_prepaint_measure` for text measurement and emit a text draw command; its string
is copied into frame storage. This does not construct an arbitrary component tree
inside a tooltip.

The callback receives an immutable geometry/input snapshot. Use writer operations
for paint, measurement and `cui_prepaint_request_frame` when another animation
frame is needed. Do not create boxes, modify focus, dispatch input, invalidate a
context or register additional callbacks inside it. Read-only queries are allowed,
but the snapshot provides the relevant current-frame geometry. Existing input
routing still uses the published dispatch graph; this hook does not replace it.

Payload bytes are copied with alignment. Pointers inside them remain borrowed.
Image and mesh storage must remain valid until rendering completes; the writer
itself exists only during the callback. CUI applies inherited opacity once, so
callbacks should not multiply it again.

A false callback or invalid emission discards that callback's output. Draw-capacity
failure empties the final draw list and reports `CUI_ERROR_CAPACITY`. This guarantee
does not roll back side effects in application code. Configure bounded element,
command and frame-storage capacities for the workload and handle registration
failure. Prepaint adds per-context storage; the detailed capacity and scaling
measurements are recorded in the development validation report.

Headless tests cover copying, clipping, ordering, opacity, capacity and mutation
guards. An actual WASM browser fixture covers stationary-pointer scroll/resize,
passive/modal occlusion and deferred pixels. These are Core-hook tests; a component
must explicitly integrate the hook to gain those current-frame behaviors.

documents.docs/DATE_PICKER_ACTIONS.md:
# Date-only DatePicker actions

Include `cui/cui_date_picker_actions.h` and rebuild with matching sources and
headers. This opt-in facade adds bounded presets and clear actions. Existing
DatePicker APIs and layouts are unchanged. It is not date-time editing, an event
observer, arbitrary styling or a complete DatePicker parity claim. The canonical library and generated distributions include both implementation
files. The focused tests compile and execute the example below.

This Base composition is compiled by the private fixture. Call it once inside a
frame; state and value persist across frames. Use a real host viewport rather
than a clock or platform date service in the widget.

```c
#include "cui/cui_date_picker_actions.h"
#include <stdio.h>

/* Small custom numeric Calendar control grid. It deliberately has no weekday
 * columns: each date/month/year is an explicitly labeled button. */
static void example_calendar_paint(cui_context *ui, cui_id root, size_t first) {
    size_t count;
    const cui_semantic *sem = cui_semantics(ui, &count);
    unsigned column = 0;
    cui_begin_box(ui, 0, (cui_box){.row = true});
    for (size_t i = first; i < count; i++) {
        if (sem[i].role != CUI_ROLE_BUTTON)
            continue;
        if (column == 7) {
            cui_end_box(ui);
            cui_begin_box(ui, 0, (cui_box){.row = true});
            column = 0;
        }
        char number[16];
        cui_str label = sem[i].name;
        if (sem[i].value > 0) {
            snprintf(number, sizeof number, "%.0f", (double)sem[i].value);
            label = cui_string(number);
        }
        const char *navigation[] = {"<<", "<", ">", ">>", "Year"};
        for (unsigned part = 1; part <= 5; part++)
            if (sem[i].id == cui_calendar_navigation_id(root, part))
                label = cui_string(navigation[part - 1]);
        if (sem[i].id == cui_calendar_month_toggle_id(root))
            label = cui_string("Month");
        cui_begin_box(ui, sem[i].id, (cui_box){.width = 40, .height = 32,
            .clip = true, .border_width = cui_focused(ui) == sem[i].id ? 2 : 0,
            .border = {255, 255, 255, 255},
            .background = sem[i].checked ? (cui_color){30, 100, 180, 255}
                                       : (cui_color){30, 30, 30, 255}});
        cui_text(ui, label, 12, sem[i].disabled ? (cui_color){100, 100, 100, 255}
                                             : (cui_color){255, 255, 255, 255});
        cui_end_box(ui);
        column++;
    }
    cui_end_box(ui);
}

void example_date_actions(cui_context *ui, cui_date_picker_actions_state *state,
                          cui_date_picker_value *value, cui_rect viewport) {
    /* Initialize once before the first frame:
     * cui_date_picker_actions_init(state, 702, (cui_date){2024, 2, 1});
     * *value = (cui_date_picker_value){.dates = {.start = {2024, 2, 15}}}; */
    const cui_date_picker_preset preset = {
        .id = 704, .label = {"March", 5},
        .value = {.dates = {.start = {2024, 3, 1}}}};
    cui_date_picker_actions_options options = {
        .picker = {.viewport = viewport},
        .calendar = {.presentation = {.month_toggle = true}},
        .presets = &preset, .preset_count = 1,
        .clear_id = 703, .clear_label = {"Clear", 5}, .cleanable = true,
        .delete_policy = CUI_DATE_PICKER_DELETE_TRIGGER};
    cui_date_picker_actions_result result = cui_base_date_picker_actions(
        ui, 701, 700, cui_string("Date"), state, value, options);
    if (!result.valid)
        return;
    cui_begin_box(ui, 701, (cui_box){.width = 180, .height = 32});
    cui_text(ui, cui_string("Choose date"), 14, (cui_color){255, 255, 255, 255});
    cui_end_box(ui);
    if (options.cleanable && cui_date_valid(value->dates.start)) {
        cui_begin_box(ui, 703, (cui_box){.width = 80, .height = 32});
        cui_text(ui, options.clear_label, 14, (cui_color){255, 255, 255, 255});
        cui_end_box(ui);
    }
    if (state->picker.overlay.open) {
        cui_rect panel = {viewport.x, viewport.y + 40, 300, 360};
        cui_overlay_set_bounds(&state->picker.overlay, panel);
        if (cui_overlay_begin(ui, &state->picker.overlay)) {
            cui_begin_portal(ui, 702, (cui_box){.clip = true}, panel, 100);
            cui_calendar_options calendar = options.picker.calendar;
            calendar.range = state->calendar_value.shape == CUI_DATE_PICKER_RANGE;
            calendar.disabled = calendar.disabled || options.picker.disabled;
            /* Capture the semantic range, submit once, then custom-paint its
             * numeric buttons without reducing input again. */
            size_t calendar_first;
            cui_semantics(ui, &calendar_first);
            cui_calendar_submit_ex(ui, 700, &state->picker.calendar,
                state->calendar_value.dates, calendar, options.calendar.presentation);
            example_calendar_paint(ui, 700, calendar_first);
            cui_date_picker_actions_submit_presets(ui, state, options);
            cui_begin_box(ui, 704, (cui_box){.width = 180, .height = 32});
            cui_text(ui, preset.label, 14, (cui_color){255, 255, 255, 255});
            cui_end_box(ui);
            cui_end_box(ui);
            cui_overlay_end(ui);
        }
    }
}
```

`cui_calendar_submit_ex` submits semantics, not layout/paint. The example paints
a simple numeric button grid including navigation and selectors; it is not a
weekday-aligned Calendar design. Use the Styled facade for the standard Calendar
layout or implement a custom Base layout for all submitted IDs.
The preset helper submits semantics only, inside the caller's one popup scope;
it does not begin/end a scope, paint, reduce input or invoke predicates/callbacks.
Use the same valid options as the successful Base call and paint each submitted
preset ID within the visible panel. Do not call it twice. It publishes copied
submission identities only after every interaction submission succeeds. Its state
argument is mutable solely for that bounded metadata. No borrowed pointer is
retained. A helper failure cannot turn a partial list into next-frame eligibility.

The new state deliberately keeps the outer picker value and child Calendar value
separate. Initialize once with `cui_date_picker_actions_init`; NULL is a no-op.
The first successful call synchronizes the supplied outer value once. Later
external value changes are detected by comparing dates/shape with `observed_value`.
A rejected child update is not retried on ordinary render, reopening or a matcher
change alone. Do not edit internal observed/submission fields or manually toggle
the overlay; use ordinary trigger/dismiss input. Calendar view/cursor remain
caller-owned, like the existing picker. Reinitialization invalidates all identities.

SOURCE presets accept the outer value first, including shape changes, then attempt
child synchronization. Child eligibility uses CUI bounds and the pure disabled
predicate; Calendar-only `disabled` does not reject assigned values. Rejection
leaves child selection/month/view/range phase unchanged. Acceptance sets the child
selection and range phase, and recenters month/year to the start without resetting
view/year page/cursors. These are the frozen source's independent ownership and
accepted-start recenter semantics, with explicit CUI min/max extension. Presets
close and unconditionally request trigger focus. An accepted complete child day activation publishes the child's value/shape back
to the outer value even if the child date did not change: upstream Calendar emits
Selected for this case. Actual child edits also publish incomplete ranges under
CUI's existing partial-range semantics; this is not a claim of upstream's
completion-only subscription delivery. Navigation and rejected activation do not
overwrite a diverged outer value.

MATCH_SHAPE rejects mismatched preset shapes but otherwise keeps SOURCE child
semantics. MATCH_SHAPE_ENABLED additionally atomically rejects a preset if either
endpoint is disabled/out of bounds. No interior range scan occurs. Relative dates
are resolved by the host. `picker.calendar.range` is ignored by this facade;
the outer value and independent child each carry explicit shape.

Clear empties both values, keeps outer shape, resets the child's range phase,
closes without an explicit focus request, and preserves month/year/view/cursors.
Empty candidates never call the date predicate. `cleanable` controls visibility;
Delete is separately OFF (default), TRIGGER or SCOPE. Only unmodified nonrepeating
Delete is handled; Backspace is not. SCOPE covers prior submitted enabled
Calendar navigation/month/year headers, grids/day cells and preset buttons.
Outer disabled gates every action; Calendar-only disabled gates day input.
Preset buttons match the source's disabled Tab-stop policy; pointer, accessibility
and explicit focus plus Enter activation remain available. The existing modal
outside-dismiss policy still applies to trigger-row controls outside an open
popup: dismiss first, then activate clear, or use scope Delete. This slice does
not change Core hit routing to promise one-click outside-popup clear.
There is no future text-entry child in this date-only slice.

Preflight rejects all invalid options before mutation, event consumption or
predicates. Limit8 presets and96 UTF-8 bytes per nonempty label; no embedded NUL,
invalid scalar, zero/duplicate/conflicting IDs or invalid dates. Single end must
be absent; range can be empty/start-only/ordered complete, never end-only.
Presets are complete. Active clear support needs a distinct nonzero ID and label.
Viewport must be finite/positive; width and cell size finite/nonnegative, with
zero retaining the existing defaults and small positive cells using normalization.
Bounds must be absent or valid and ordered. Unknown action enums fail; inherited
Calendar extension normalization remains its existing contract. Styled also
requires finite positive font size/nonnegative padding/gap/radius and enough
viewport width/height for all preset rows; it does not submit clipped-off rows.
Ordinary global caller ID collision discipline remains required, including custom
Base layouts. Known Calendar ID conflicts are rejected before predicate calls.

The array and preset values are borrowed for the call; label/locale strings and
predicate/user resources remain valid through normal frame/render consumption.
Do not mutate UI from predicates. State/value belong to the owner UI thread. Reinitialize before using the state
with a different context; frame sequences are local to one context.
No resources are allocated or retained by this API. It is not a stable binary
layout extension to old records; rebuild new state users with matching headers.

Queue order is the existing selector Escape prepass, overlay dismissal/trigger
pass, one entire Calendar reduction, then one action pass in frame event order.
Only the first eligible accepted action wins. Rejected presets do not consume.
Calendar and action phases are not global chronological interleaving: a queued
preset can run after a later queued day edit. Calendar auto-close in that one
reduction does not erase original submitted action eligibility; prior overlay
dismissal does erase it; trigger toggles and Escape
invalidate popup action eligibility for the call. Prior records expire after an
omitted frame, omitted/failed popup submission, or a close/reopen transition.
They contain only copied IDs/grid/week-start/month-count/frame sequence and at
most 144 target IDs for submitted enabled Calendar/preset targets,
not a host/context pointer. Scope Delete additionally checks current view/grid
and list membership; a reserved but absent/off-view year/day ID cannot qualify.
The 144 bound includes at most 126 day occurrences, navigation
and the 8 presets; the helper publishes no partial target set. A real previous
semantic/input target is still required;
retained IDs alone cannot fabricate an activation. Current preset ID and policy
must also match. Same-frame root reuse and global ID collisions remain invalid.

`valid` denotes preflight success; later Core capacity/layout errors are not
transaction rollback. `calendar` is the single Calendar reduction result;
`action_activated` is acceptance independently of a value edit; `changed` compares
final outer dates/shape with the entry value. Clear on empty and identical presets
can be accepted without `changed`. No typed observer wrapper or binding builder
is added here. No live AT, browser or platform date-time behavior is implied.

Scope Delete additionally requires current view/list eligibility. A submitted year
then hidden by a day-view switch, or a day hidden by a month change, is rejected.
Unsubmitted year/day IDs are not retained. Core may redirect programmatic focus
on an unknown ID to a valid modal target before dispatch; Delete then applies to
that actual target, not to the unknown requested ID. The popup root itself is not
a Scope Delete target.


## Verified worker fixture

The isolated fixture registers the new sources without changing canonical build
metadata. Strict C11 (`-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror`) and the C++ header compile
pass. The guide code block is extracted, compiled and exercised by the test.

```sh
cmake -S tests/fixtures/date_picker_actions -B build-actions -G Ninja
cmake --build build-actions --target test_date_picker_actions date_picker_actions_header date_picker_actions_guide test_picker test_picker_base test_picker_events test_date_picker_calendar test_calendar_behavior test_calendar_presentation
python scripts/test_local.py --build-dir build-actions -- -V -R '^(date_picker_actions|picker|picker_base_only|picker_events|date_picker_calendar|calendar_behavior|calendar_presentation)$'
```

Results: native 7/7; separate `-DCUI_SANITIZE=ON` build, ASan/UBSan 7/7.
The action suite reports 1,738 assertions for Base and Styled, including source
child-rejection/retention, complete no-op adoption, clear/preset focus, queue order,
preflight failures, previous-submission expiry, off-view target rejection,
reduce-once and legacy zero-option semantic/software-pixel equivalence.

The private `tests/fixtures/date_picker_actions/test_wasi.py --build-dir BUILD`
uses the existing repository pinned WASI driver: Zig 0.16.0, wasmtime 49.0.0,
WASI Preview 1. It passed 1/1 suite and the same 1,738 assertions, with source
fingerprint validation. No dependencies were installed. Native checks used the
private-display wrapper; no browser, desktop, live AT or bindings were exercised.
Canonical source/umbrella/amalgamation/binding/metadata registration, generated
skill resources and full integration checks remain the integrator's work.

documents.docs/DOCK.md:
# Styled Dock

The Dock view composes the portable Dock model, Tabs, Buttons and Resizable into a themed workspace. Each leaf shows an overflow-scrolling tab strip and calls only its active panel's content renderer. Nested horizontal and vertical splits use pointer and keyboard resize handles; changed ratios or opt-in n-ary slot sizes are written back to the model and therefore included in its serialization.

This implementation was checked against the frozen GPUI Kit 0.7.1 reference, commit `87d10ae5e1299d1be18670a37570c5402f952e34`, including `crates/component/src/dock/dock.rs`, `tab_panel.rs` and `website/component/dock.md`. It implements a specific subset, not full Dock family parity. Panel menus are implemented. Native floating windows are outside the frozen Dock API audited here. Edge regions have explicit limits described below. Programmatic `cui_dock_move` and `cui_dock_split` remain available. No platform or Clay types appear in the public API.

## Minimal composition

```c
#include "cui/cui_dock.h"

static void editor(cui_context *ui, uint64_t key, void *user) {
    (void)key;
    const cui_theme *theme = user;
    cui_text(ui, cui_string("Your editor controls go here"), 16, theme->text);
}

/* Create once, outside the frame loop. */
cui_dock *model = cui_dock_create((cui_dock_config){0});
cui_dock_view *view = cui_dock_view_create(0, 0);
/* Check both allocations before use. Panel keys are stable application IDs. */
cui_dock_insert(model, cui_dock_root(model), 42, 0);

/* Inside a begun CUI frame; theme and registry stay alive through rendering. */
cui_dock_panel registry[] = {
    {.key = 42, .title = cui_string("Editor"), .closable = true,
     .render = editor, .user = &theme}
};
cui_dock_view_options options = cui_dock_view_options_default();
options.width = available_width;
options.height = available_height;
cui_dock_view_result result = cui_dock_view_render(
    ui, 100, &theme, model, view, registry, 1, options);
/* Check result.error. Destroy view and model after the frame loop. */
```

`width` and `height` must be finite and positive. Content callbacks submit ordinary CUI controls inside a clipped active-panel region; the host provides frame/event processing and rendering. Registry keys must be unique. An absent registry entry produces an unavailable-panel placeholder rather than invoking a callback. Panel keys include zero and `UINT64_MAX`. Panel-key-derived tab and close IDs remain stable across reorder and persistence restore; node-derived group/handle IDs change when model generations change.

View storage is allocated once, with defaults of 128 nodes and 256 panels. Its capacities must cover the entire model; insufficient capacity returns `CUI_DOCK_CAPACITY` before submitting the view. Keep one retained view per model/workspace ID. Rebinding to another model or ID resets retained state; `cui_dock_view_reset` explicitly clears it. Registry strings, callback user data and retained view storage must remain valid through frame rendering.

## Interaction and state

- Clicking a tab selects its stable panel key. Tabs provide roving focus, arrow/Home/End navigation and overflow reveal; disabled tabs are skipped.
- A closable, enabled panel has a real close button. Ctrl/Meta+W closes the active panel when its tab or close button has focus, outside IME composition. Closing collapses empty nonroot groups through the model and restores focus to a surviving tab.
- Same-group pointer drag reorders tabs without changing their panel keys or active selection. `allow_reorder` disables this. Cross-group drops in the center append and select the panel in the target group. Content edges create a 50/50 horizontal or vertical split; the tab strip itself accepts center moves. Edge zones extend up to 32 logical pixels (20% for small groups); left/right take precedence at corners. A six-pixel pointer movement starts the gesture. Escape, pointer cancellation, dropping outside the workspace, disabled sources and disabled workspaces cancel it.
- Split handles use existing Resizable pointer capture, keyboard stepping, focus and semantics. Aggregate child minimum sizes constrain feasible layouts; infeasible minima are proportionally compressed and reported by `result.fit_minimum == false`. Split handles overlay the zero-gap model layout rather than consuming a separate gutter.
- `focus_content` optionally moves focus to the selected panel's declared `focus_target` after its callback submits controls. With this option disabled, selection keeps tab focus.
- `disabled` prevents workspace interactions and marks content inert. The panel callback still renders its contents.

Selection and resizing update the model during the call. Structural close/reorder/move/split changes commit after scene submission and invalidate the next frame; redraw before inspecting their new geometry. At most one structural action commits per view call, with close taking precedence over a cross-group/edge drop, then same-group reorder. Rejected drops return a model error and preserve the layout; the initial pointer-down selection remains an ordinary selection. `on_event` receives selected/closed/reordered/resized/moved/split notifications; callbacks must not mutate or destroy the model/view during rendering. Do external model edits between frames. Serialization persists model layout, active keys and ratios, not registry callbacks or transient view state; see [Dock model](DOCK_MODEL.md).

`cui_dock_split_layout` exposes the same pure split geometry/minimum calculation used by model layout. It accepts a split node and finite nonnegative rectangle/gap, returns its two child rectangles and aggregate minima, and leaves output untouched on failure. Its `fit_minimum` reports fit along the split axis; the view's result reports the whole workspace's two-dimensional minimum fit.

During a drag, an inert title preview follows the pointer inside the workspace and an outlined translucent region shows a center or half-group edge target. Default colors come from the supplied theme. `custom_palette` enables `drag_palette.accepted`, `rejected`, `background` and `text`, including alpha. `disable_preview` suppresses these portals; `disabled` suppresses gestures and previews. Preview geometry changes immediately without animation, including when `reduced_motion` is true. The underlying Tabs adapter retains its own same-group insertion marker. The target indicates host-policy permission; model/view capacity can still reject the release.

`allow_operation(policy_user, request)` is a reusable veto for `CUI_DOCK_CLOSED`, `MOVED`, `SPLIT` and `REORDERED`. The request carries stable panel/source IDs and operation-specific destination/index/axis/before fields. For close there is no destination; for reorder `index` is the final index and `source` identifies the group. Move uses `SIZE_MAX` to append; split uses a 50/50 ratio. NULL allows every operation. The callback must be pure and must not mutate the model/view: it can run during preview and runs again immediately before commit. A denial sets `result.denied`, emits no structural event and leaves layout unchanged; normal pointer-down tab selection may already have occurred. Programmatic model calls intentionally bypass view policies, allowing the host to implement its own trusted actions.

`cui_dock_move_split` stages removal and split creation in the model's bounded scratch storage and commits only on success. Rejection preserves layout, node generations and output. The new leaf inherits the source group's minimum sizes; target constraints remain unchanged. A sole panel cannot split itself. View storage must accommodate two additional nodes for an edge drop (a conservative check even when a source collapse could free nodes).

For generated standalone distribution use, `CUI_DOCK_BASE_ONLY` opts out of the styled Dock implementation so the pure model can be embedded without the component library. Define it before `CUI_DOCK_IMPLEMENTATION` and including `dist/cui_dock.h`. The normal styled bundle requires its documented CUI component dependencies; this macro does not remove public model declarations.

## Active-panel toolbars and zoom

An optional `cui_dock_panel.toolbar` callback submits ordinary controls in a clipped row below the tab strip. It receives the same key/user data as content. Only the active panel in each visible leaf runs this callback. `toolbar_height` defaults to 32 logical pixels when the row is present; content receives the remaining height. Disabled panels/workspaces render the toolbar inert.

`panel.zoomable` exposes a maximize control. Its stable ID is `cui_dock_zoom_id(view_id, panel_key)`. A view retains a stable maximized-panel key and renders its leaf across the workspace, hiding other split leaves. Tab selection within that leaf updates the retained key. Zoom changes presentation, not split ratios or model serialization. `cui_dock_view_zoomed` queries it; `cui_dock_view_set_zoom` allows trusted host actions and activates the requested panel. Zoom-in consults `allow_operation` with `CUI_DOCK_ZOOMED` and emits that event on success. Restore emits `CUI_DOCK_RESTORED` and is always available while maximized, even when zoom capability, policy or workspace interactivity is later revoked. It bypasses veto to avoid trapping the user. Restoring requests focus on the panel tab; disabled tabs follow the core's normal focus eligibility rules.

Node generations can change during model restore while the maximized key survives. Removing the maximized panel automatically restores the workspace and requests a surviving tab's focus on the next frame. View reset clears zoom. Toolbar callbacks and all policy/event callbacks must respect the rendering ownership contract.

## Independently resizable edge regions

`cui_dock_workspace` wraps four independent model/view/registry descriptors ordered `CENTER`, `LEFT`, `RIGHT`, `BOTTOM`. Left/right run the full workspace height; bottom sits below the center between the side regions. Each open region can itself contain nested splits and tabs. Stable `cui_dock_region_id` values namespace controls, and `cui_dock_edge_resize_id` exposes keyboard-focusable resize handles.

```c
cui_dock_workspace *area = cui_dock_workspace_create();
/* Create and check distinct models/views for every region you will open. */
cui_dock_region_content regions[4] = {
    {.model = center_model, .view = center_view,
     .panels = center_registry, .panel_count = center_count},
    {.model = left_model, .view = left_view,
     .panels = left_registry, .panel_count = left_count},
    {.model = right_model, .view = right_view,
     .panels = right_registry, .panel_count = right_count},
    {.model = bottom_model, .view = bottom_view,
     .panels = bottom_registry, .panel_count = bottom_count}
};
cui_dock_workspace_set_edge(area, CUI_DOCK_LEFT, true, 200);
cui_dock_workspace_set_edge(area, CUI_DOCK_BOTTOM, true, 160);
/* Within a frame; an unopened descriptor may be empty. */
cui_dock_workspace_options area_options = {
    .view = cui_dock_view_options_default()
};
area_options.view.width = available_width;
area_options.view.height = available_height;
cui_dock_workspace_result area_result = cui_dock_workspace_render(
    ui, 100, &theme, area, regions, area_options);
/* Destroy area and separately destroy all owned models/views when done. */
```

Use `cui_dock_workspace_set_edge` between frames to open/close a region and assign its preferred size; a closed region runs no panel callbacks. Actual sizes respect aggregate model minima and optional region minima, defaulting to 64 logical pixels for center and 48 for edges. Infeasible minima are proportionally compressed inside the viewport and reported by `fit_minimum == false`. Preferred sizes survive closing and temporary viewport compression. Real handle adjustments update them and invoke `on_resize` with the edge and new logical-pixel size. Disabled/motion settings propagate to all handles and views. The wrapper performs descriptor/capacity validation before submitting its root. Configured region models/views must be distinct, including closed regions prepared as drop destinations.

`cui_dock_workspace_save` and `restore` copy a portable field-based snapshot containing three open flags, preferred sizes and noncollapsible flags. Restore validates every positive finite size before changing state. Encode these fields in the host's persistence format alongside each region model's ordinary serialization; the snapshot is not a specified binary file format. Model callbacks, panel registries and transient focus/drag state are not serialized. Mutate neither workspace state nor region models from render/policy/resize callbacks.

Zoom is local to the selected region's view; other edge regions remain present. Drag/drop can transfer between configured region models as described below. The wrapper supplies layout and callbacks, not built-in toggle menus or native floating windows.

## Cross-region transfer and closed-edge landing zones

A workspace observes tab drags using clipped core focus-scope hits. Dropping into another visible region appends/selects the panel, or creates a 50/50 leaf when released at a content edge. A closed left/right/bottom region has an outer landing zone up to 24 logical pixels (20% of a small viewport), with left/right precedence at corners. A successful drop inserts into its first leaf and opens the edge using its retained preferred size. An empty closed model is a valid destination. Opening commits after transfer; cancellation, disabled input, policy rejection, duplicates or capacity failure never open it. `disable_cross_region` suppresses these transfers; `disable_edge_opening` removes closed-edge landing zones. Regular in-region reordering/splitting retains its existing behavior. Modals/higher interactive layers block workspace gestures through core hit eligibility.

Both source and destination descriptors must already register enabled metadata for the panel key. Models are separate containers; registries, strings, callback user data, views and models remain host-owned and must survive the frame/renderer and notifications. Nothing copies/frees registry data. After success, only the destination active content callback runs for that key; duplicate panel keys in the destination are rejected. Internal Dock IDs are namespaced by region, but callback-created control IDs must still be globally unique in the CUI frame.

`cui_dock_transfer` stages source removal and destination insertion/splitting in each model's bounded scratch storage, then swaps both states only on success. Failure preserves both serialized layouts, node generations and output. Split leaves inherit source-group minima. Ordinary model APIs and direct transfer calls bypass host view policies. Workspace gestures also restore the source group's pre-drag active key on cross-region rejection/cancellation, so dragging an inactive tab cannot leave a serialization change behind. If incidental selection occurred, the view emits a corrective `CUI_DOCK_SELECTED` notification for the restored key; no transfer notification is emitted. Ordinary clicks and same-group gestures retain their selection behavior.

The source view's `allow_operation` checks MOVED/SPLIT intent. Its destination node belongs to the other model; use workspace `allow_transfer(transfer_user, request)` for policies needing both models/region IDs. This pure callback can also run during target preview and cannot mutate model/view/workspace state. A denial sets workspace `result.denied`. `on_transfer` runs after both models commit and receives source/destination regions/models, stable key, final destination leaf and whether the edge opened. The request pointer is borrowed only during the callback; the source node ID can be stale after removal collapses its former group. Redraw before inspecting new geometry; destination tab focus is requested on the following frame, or the destination's `focus_target` when `focus_content` is enabled. Zoomed destination views reject drops, matching the frozen reference's zoomed-group safeguard.

The frozen reference distinguishes panel drops (`base/src/dock/tab_group.rs`) from dock resize listeners (`component/src/dock/dock.rs`). This implementation supports panel-drop opening of configured closed edges. The opt-in collapsed bottom strip also supports handle-driven reopening as documented below; no native floating-window behavior is implied. Accepted/rejected cross-region landing indicators use the same custom palette and preview settings as the view; actual model capacity remains checked at release.

The Dock clips callback paint/input to each allocated pane. A nested GROW callback box with a long intrinsic line can still report raw bounds wider than that pane; those bounds do not enlarge the pane or allow hits into its neighbor. Hosts displaying semantic geometry should use clipped visible bounds or cap/wrap their own content boxes. Headless oversized-content tests verify pane allocation and clipped hover isolation.

## Panel action menus

Each active leaf displays an SVG ellipsis menu button by default; `hide_menu` opts out. The adapter composes the existing Menu tree reducer, popup painter and ghost icon buttons. The toolbar zoom button is also an SVG icon with an accessible action name. Custom borrowed `cui_menu_entry` trees are supplied through `panel.menu_items/menu_count`; selected custom IDs invoke `panel.menu_action` after scene submission using `panel.user`. Trees must have unique nonzero interactive IDs, valid descriptors and at most `CUI_DOCK_CUSTOM_MENU_LIMIT` entries across all descendants. Builtin action IDs are reserved. Keep descriptors and callback data alive across frames; do not mutate model/view state from callbacks.

Builtins provide Close, Close Others, Close All, Maximize/Restore, horizontal/vertical split and Balance parent split. Close Others/All operate on the current leaf and skip protected, disabled or unregistered panels. Every eligible key passes the close policy before an atomic bulk removal; one veto preserves the complete serialized layout. `result.closed_count` reports the number removed and each successful close emits its usual notification. `cui_dock_remove_many` exposes the same transactional model primitive without view policies. Split moves the active tab into a new 50/50 leaf and requires at least two tabs; Balance sets the immediate parent ratio to 0.5. Existing split/resize policies apply. Disabled actions cannot activate, while restore stays reachable for an already maximized panel. `cui_dock_menu_id` and `cui_dock_menu_action_id` provide stable semantic IDs.

The frozen component `dock/tab_panel.rs` composes host dropdown entries with zoom and close actions, using ellipsis and maximize/minimize SVG controls. CUI follows that composition; Close Others/All, split and balance are explicit CUI extensions, not claims about the frozen menu. Customizable zoom-control placement and native floating hosting remain separate work.

## Validation

The headless styled test exercises active-only callbacks, stable keys including zero/`UINT64_MAX`, disabled controls, keyboard focus/navigation, close shortcuts, collapse, same-group pointer reorder, nested pointer/keyboard resize, infeasible minima, restore/reset and capacity rejection. The model unit test checks split geometry against leaf layout and transactional validation failures. Cross-group and all four edge drops are tested with queued pointer events, including cancellation, outside drops, disabled/reorder-off and full-capacity rejection. Draw-command tests verify accepted/rejected custom preview palettes, preview suppression, motion settings and cancellation cleanup. Host veto tests cover close buttons/shortcuts, reorder, move and split. Toolbar/zoom tests cover active callbacks, controls, focus, persistence and removal recovery. Edge-region tests cover independent geometry, real pointer/keyboard resize, callbacks, hide/reopen, model minima, disabled controls, constrained viewport preference retention and rejected snapshot restoration. Two-model transfer tests cover queued region/edge gestures, callback ownership/focus, empty closed destinations, cancellation/modal blocking, duplicate keys, disabled/missing metadata, policy veto and full model capacities, with serialized/snapshot equality on rejection. Menu tests cover SVG draw commands, custom and disabled actions, all four zoom placements, restore, atomic bulk vetoes, protected tabs and split/balance actions. Collapsed-bottom tests exercise real pointer/keyboard opening and snapping, same-tab reopening, protected close shortcuts, no content/actions while closed, disabled gestures and snapshot rollback on cancellation or a blocking modal. No native window or browser-specific behavior was tested for this adapter.

