# DropdownButton paired notifications

`cui_base_dropdown_button_events` and `cui_dropdown_button_events` add synchronous observers for the primary action and individual menu commands. They preserve the paired DropdownButton presentation and legacy aggregate result; no separate SplitButton family, new queue or binding facade is introduced. [MenuTree events](MENU_EVENTS.md), [control events](CONTROL_EVENTS.md) and [ContextMenu events](CONTEXT_MENU_EVENTS.md) describe the reused payload types.

```c
static void action(void *user, const cui_component_event_metadata *event) {
    /* ACTIVATE, target == action_id; copied accepted Core evidence. */
    (void)user; (void)event;
}
static void command(void *user, const cui_menu_command *event) {
    /* ACTIVATE, event.target == trigger_id; command is the leaf ID. */
    *(cui_id *)user = event->command;
}
cui_dropdown_event_result result = cui_dropdown_button_events(
    ui, container, action_id, trigger_id, &theme,
    cui_string("Save"), cui_string("More actions"), entries, count,
    &menu_action, &menu, options,
    (cui_dropdown_event_handlers){{action, NULL}, {command, &last_command}});
```

The Base facade takes the legacy Base arguments followed by `cui_dropdown_event_handlers`. Its zero action/trigger ID omits that half; both zero and equal IDs remain invalid. Styled uses the existing `hide_action`/`hide_menu` options. `container` is paint identity, not an invented activation target.

`cui_dropdown_event_result` contains `valid`, `submitted`, `count_overflow`, `action_count`, `command_count` and `legacy` (`cui_dropdown_button_result`). Both counts mean actual callback invocations: a missing observer counts zero while normal activation, writes and popup closing continue. Counts saturate without stopping dispatch. The two legacy booleans preserve their old aggregate meanings; they do not establish how many events occurred. This differs from Button's facade activation-attempt count.

The existing order is preserved: **Base action reduction → action observation → Base menu reduction/observation → Styled role updates, painting, retained metrics and tooltip composition**. Base Button is called once. The observer pass passively enumerates actual accepted activations after that registration and copies each Core activation snapshot before calling user code. It neither re-runs Button nor reconstructs events from its aggregate bool. MenuTree's real per-command commit seam runs once, preserving check/radio writes, keep-open multiplicity and frozen closing-command exit decisions. Trigger opening emits no menu leaf notification.

Action metadata targets `action_id`, includes genuine accepted evidence, and preserves mouse/touch/key/accessibility source and frame/index. Menu metadata targets `trigger_id`; any copied activation retains its actual child target. MenuTree's ordinary reducer-before-registration path has no copied activation snapshot. No programmatic evidence is synthesized. Action phase precedes menu phase even if the action's queued event index is larger; callbacks are not sorted, replayed or deferred. An action observer may update caller values or consume events before menu reduction.

The normal Styled root menu painter creates a modal focus scope, even if `state.overlay.modal` was changed. Consequently an open Styled popup does not independently admit outside primary-action input. The fixture proves mixed action/menu ordering with a genuine Base composition that paints menu rows in a modeless scope; it does not claim that the normal Styled popup is modeless. No scope behavior changed in this slice.

Action disabled/loading suppresses that half; loading retains existing busy/Loading semantics and visuals. Outer/menu disabled policy still suppresses both. Hidden action has no semantic or observer; hidden menu closes the popup and clears depth. Existing Link presentation updates roles only after Base reduction. A genuine accessibility ACTIVATE queued against the previous LINK role is rejected when the current Base registration is BUTTON; this existing mismatch affects legacy and new results. The new facade reports no invented callback/evidence and does not claim complete Link accessibility parity. Pointer behavior and the final Link semantic roles remain unchanged.

Handlers and payloads are borrowed only during synchronous submission. Pointer-free metadata/command copies survive frames and context destruction. Keep active descriptors, state, values, users and render/tooltip resources alive and stable through submission. Owner writes are notifications' effects, not recursive change events or vetoes. Direct mutation of in-flight navigation, freeing active storage, context destruction and recursive submission during dispatch remain unsupported; defer shutdown/removal until submission/frame completion. Existing inert/layout-only/omitted invocation behavior applies.

[test_dropdown_events.c](../tests/unit/test_dropdown_events.c) checks Base and Styled real mouse/touch/key/accessibility action acceptance, multiple actions, missing observers, busy/disabled/hidden halves, old/new geometry and custom painting, repeated keep-open commands, copied lifetimes, inert/omitted calls and Link rejection. Its Base modeless composition proves decreasing action/menu indices, applied owner writes and consumption before menu reduction; Styled proves normal modal exclusion. Existing Choice tests retain broader tooltip, anchor, variant and artwork coverage; ControlEvents and other menu fixtures protect neighboring facades.

```sh
python scripts/test_local.py --build-dir build -- -R '^(dropdown_events|choice|control_events_facades|menu_events|simple_menu_events|context_menu_events)$'
```

The integrator registers tests and documentation exports. No generated files, callback retiming, manifest changes or parity promotion are included.

documents.docs/IMMEDIATE_AND_STATE.md:
# Frames, state and callbacks

CUI uses immediate frame composition with persistent application and context state. Call components inside `cui_begin_frame` / `cui_end_frame`; they submit this frame's layout, draw commands and semantics. Keep values and long-lived models across frames. These are two parts of the same API, rather than separate immediate and retained rendering engines.

## What persists for each kind of component

| Components | Frame composition | Application state retained across frames |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Button, Label, Badge, Avatar, Separator | Call the component each frame | Usually no separate control model; keep application data such as click counts |
| Checkbox, Radio, Switch, Toggle, Slider, Rating, Pagination | Submit with the current value | Boolean, enum, number or selected page; animated controls may also use an explicit state struct |
| Input, Textarea, Editor model | Submit an editable control | `cui_edit` and its application-owned buffer; styled input/textarea behavior and layout state |
| Dialog, AlertDialog, Sheet, Popover | Submit while its surface is active | `cui_surface_state`, open state, decision result and any form data |
| Select, Combobox, Menu | Submit trigger and current popup contents | Choice/menu state, selected values and query editor where applicable |
| DataTable, Tree, Chart, TextView, MessageScroller | Compose visible content from the model | Data, keys, selection and the explicit model/state required by the public header |
| VirtualList, Dock, Plot infrastructure | Use the model to compute geometry/ranges for a frame | Virtual-list/dock handles or application-owned plot scales/data; their current gallery stories demonstrate partial base models |

A retained data model is not a retained application widget tree. CUI's context also retains focus, prior hit regions, resources and transient behavior. Destroy the context after the host stops consuming its draw lists; destroy separately owned models according to their headers.

The table describes ownership and submission. It does not claim that every entry has reached complete reference behavior. The gallery's Implementation scope lists the remaining features.

## Handle return-value events

Many controls report a change or activation by returning `true`. Invoke your application function in that branch:

```c
/* Inside an active frame; ui, theme, save_id and disabled are supplied. */
if (cui_button(ui, save_id, &theme, cui_string("Save"), disabled))
    save_document(application);

if (cui_checkbox(ui, notify_id, &theme, cui_string("Notifications"),
                 &application->notifications, disabled))
    persist_preferences(application);
```

The button reports activation for the frame's routed input. The checkbox writes the caller's value and reports a change. Rendering another frame does not call these application functions again unless another activation/change occurs. Keep application actions out of paint-only child callbacks, which may run during layout measurement.

## Use a controlled callback

Some option structs expose `on_change`. For Switch and Toggle, a supplied callback receives the requested value; the component leaves the authoritative application value to your code. Without the callback, the control updates its value pointer directly.

```c
#include "cui/cui_components.h"

typedef struct {
    bool sync_enabled;
    cui_switch_state sync_motion;
} preferences;

static void request_sync(void *user, bool requested) {
    preferences *app = user;
    app->sync_enabled = requested;
    /* Persist or validate here, using the application's own services. */
}

void draw_sync(cui_context *ui, const cui_theme *theme,
               preferences *app, double now_seconds) {
    cui_switch_ex(ui, cui_id_from(ui, "sync"), theme,
        cui_string("Automatic sync"), &app->sync_enabled,
        &app->sync_motion,
        (cui_switch_options){.on_change = request_sync,
                             .change_user = app,
                             .now_seconds = now_seconds});
}
```

Zero-initialize `preferences` before use and retain it between frames. The callback is synchronous; keep its user pointer valid during submission. A controlled callback may reject a request by keeping the old value. The next frame submits the accepted value.

## Connect host events

The host queues `cui_event` values before a frame. CUI routes them using previous-frame regions, focus, capture and overlay scopes. Your app submits controls for the new frame, obtains its draw list and semantic tree, then paints and bridges semantics through the chosen adapters.

Use [core lifecycle](CORE.md) for event ordering and [host interfaces](INTERFACES.md) for renderer/platform callbacks. A platform callback such as clipboard access is different from a component callback such as `on_change`; the host supplies the former, and application behavior supplies the latter.

## Schedule redraws

After application state changes outside a component, call `cui_invalidate`. Hosts redraw when `cui_needs_redraw` is true and honor component-specific animation deadlines. Pass real elapsed time and system reduced-motion preferences through the host. Avoid rerunning application side effects solely because an animation paints another frame.

documents.docs/MENU_EVENTS.md:
# MenuTree command notifications

`cui_base_menu_tree_events` and `cui_menu_tree_events` add synchronous per-command ACTIVATE observers. They preserve Base/Styled MenuTree mutation, navigation and close behavior. Simple Menu, ContextMenu, Dropdown, SplitButton and other forwarding are outside this slice; no uniform menu-family or binding coverage is implied.

```c
#include <cui/cui_choice.h>

static void command_activated(void *user, const cui_menu_command *command) {
    cui_id *last_seen = user;
    *last_seen = command->command;
}

/* During composition; action/tree/descriptors and pointed-to values belong to app. */
static cui_menu_command_result file_menu(cui_context *ui, const cui_theme *theme,
    const cui_menu_entry *entries, size_t count, cui_id *action,
    cui_menu_tree_state *tree, cui_id *last_seen) {
    return cui_menu_tree_events(ui, 2, theme, cui_string("File"), entries, count,
        action, tree,
        (cui_menu_tree_options){.popup = {.viewport = {0, 0, 640, 480}},
                                .keep_open_checks = true},
        (cui_menu_command_handler){command_activated, last_seen});
}
```

Initialize tree once with `cui_menu_tree_init` and a stable nonzero popup ID. The new entry points match the legacy argument order and append `cui_menu_command_handler`. Base draws nothing; caller submits its normal menu-entry semantics/geometry after reduction. Styled retains its existing trigger, metrics and popup painting order. Descriptor/child IDs remain globally unique throughout the tree; pointed-to check/radio storage stays caller-owned.

`cui_menu_command_result` contains `valid` (applicable preflight passed), `submitted` (ordinary active submission), `command_count` (actual observer invocations), `count_overflow`, and `legacy_activated` (the original aggregate bool). Counts saturate at `SIZE_MAX` while dispatch continues. Missing observer preserves all commands, mutations and close behavior with count zero; `legacy_activated` may still be true. Disabled controls can submit but cannot command; inert/layout-only ancestry cannot notify. Later Core/rendering errors remain possible after preflight.

Every command branch reached emits once, including repeated same ID, CHECK toggled twice in one call, and RADIO confirmed while its value already matches. This is ACTIVATE, not a value CHANGE or owner-rejectable request. CHECK/RADIO writes and action output are applied before the observer; no callback return can veto them. Navigation/typeahead, submenu opening, scrolling, dismissal, labels/separators, disabled or malformed entries and external state writes do not emit command activation. Missing check/radio storage or empty submenu follows existing disabled policy.

The callback receives pointer-free `cui_menu_command { event, command }`. `command` is the actual committed leaf ID; `event.kind` is ACTIVATE and `event.target` is the public trigger. Payloads omit descriptor pointers, labels, shortcut/icon text and generic check/radio snapshots. Applications own their model and can inspect applied state synchronously. Copying the payload preserves it beyond frame/context lifetime; referenced users/state/resources remain subject to their existing lifetime boundaries.

The order at each command is existing active/depth updates, check/radio write, action output write, existing close policy, then observer. Thus callbacks see applied values and already-applied close/focus state. The reducer freezes whether that command must return before invoking the observer, preserving the closing command boundary. Keep-open commands continue through remaining eligible events in original reducer order. Observers may update command-owner values or unrelated application state and consume later input; those writes are not recursively synthesized commands. Do not perform duplicate side effects through both callback and aggregate result.

Metadata is frozen before writes/close. A genuine accepted Core activation snapshot is copied only if already available, retaining its original child target. Normal MenuTree entry semantics register after reduction, so HAS_ACTIVATION is ordinarily absent. Fallback metadata contains only actual frame/index and source proven by pointer-up, keyboard or accessibility event type. No coordinates, touch details, accepted accessibility snapshot or programmatic source is invented. Keyboard redirection can commit a different active leaf from the original routed target; command ID and any genuine snapshot each retain their actual meaning. See [shared metadata](COMPONENT_EVENTS.md).

Core already supports accessibility ACTIVATE for MENUITEM, MENUITEMCHECKBOX and MENUITEMRADIO. The tests use genuine supported menu accessibility actions; this does not remove Select/Combobox OPTION activation limitations. Touch input establishes pointer source at this seam without implying a copied touch activation snapshot.

Handler/user/payload are borrowed synchronously. Copy handler/options at call entry; descriptor arrays/children and active storage must remain stable/alive throughout submission, and rendering resources through their existing completion boundary. Directly mutating in-flight popup/navigation state, freeing active state/users/resources, destroying the active context or recursively submitting the same frame is unsupported. Request removal/shutdown in application state and perform it after submission/frame completion. The frozen return policy is not a navigation-mutation API or a runtime destruction guard. No registration, queue, routing, scheduler or foreign request hook is added.

`tests/unit/test_menu_events.c` exercises real Base/Styled commands, repeated check/radio/action IDs, post-write/post-close state, missing handlers, pointer/touch/key and supported accessibility input, nested keyboard redirection, owner updates/later consumption, closing-command termination, disabled/inert/omitted calls, legacy coexistence and copied lifetime. Existing `test_choice.c` retains navigation/depth/scroll/drag/render compatibility proof. Run registered native tests only through the invisible wrapper:

```sh
python scripts/test_local.py --build-dir build -- -R '^(menu_events|choice)$'
```

[Design checkpoint](design/MENU_COMMAND_EVENTS_SLICE.md) compares the per-command seam with the lossy aggregate-last alternative. Simple Menu/ContextMenu/Dropdown forwarding, controlled/vetoable commands, check/radio CHANGE payloads, lifecycle observers, binding exports and parity promotion remain deferred. Root owns module/CMake/generator/SDK and human/LLM API indexing integration.

Compact-ID renderer regression: RADIO entry12's old left-icon wrapper (`12 ^ 0x9e3f5d2a91bca001`) collided with SUBMENU entry15's chevron (`15 ^ 0x9e3f5d2a91bca002`), both `0x9e3f5d2a91bca00d`. The paint-only wrapper now uses anonymous row-local layout identity; named icon resource IDs and all menu routing/public IDs stay unchanged. The ordinary unit fixture uses these exact compact IDs and verifies both radio and submenu raster glyph coverage/geometry without duplicate identities. This targeted fix does not imply full Menu renderer parity.

documents.docs/PLOT_CHART_OWNER_MOTION.md:
# Owner Plot and Chart motion

`cui_plot_motion_from_theme` projects a standalone `cui_theme_motion_tokens` value into a copied `cui_plot_motion_policy`. The source projection uses the fast duration for enter/exit and pointer response, the respective enter/exit Bezier curves, source spring damping 1/travel with epsilon 0.1, and a distinct one-second quartic-out appearance. Move/control springs, move easing and rem distances do not drive Plot motion. This matches pinned 0.7.1 `87d10ae5e1299d1be18670a37570c5402f952e34`, `crates/component/src/theme/mod.rs::plot_motion`.

```c
cui_theme_motion_tokens tokens = cui_theme_motion_default();
cui_plot_motion_policy motion;
if (cui_plot_motion_from_theme(&tokens, &motion) == CUI_PLOT_OK) {
    chart_options.custom_motion = true;
    chart_options.motion = motion;
    plot_options.custom_motion = true;
    plot_options.motion = motion;
}
```

Policies contain values, including Bezier control points; they do not borrow Theme storage or contain self-referential callback userdata. Edit a copied policy for one owner and keep each owner's Chart/Plot state separate. Zero/default `custom_motion=false` preserves the existing Chart one-second appearance/120ms hover and Base Plot explicit zero-duration policies. `cui_chart_theme` continues to project colors only; it does not silently install motion.

PlotElement opt-in replaces its legacy enter/exit/appear policies. Appearance still requires its existing explicit identity/generation/frame/scope contract. Chart opt-in consumes enter/exit, appearance and pointer policy internally for line/area, bar/candlestick and pie channels. Positive legacy Chart `appear_duration` overrides policy appearance duration; positive `hover_duration` overrides enter/exit durations and pointer response. Zero legacy durations leave the explicit policy unchanged, so explicit policy duration 0/response 0 means instant. `disable_appear` and owner/Core reduced motion win. Manual reveal remains caller supplied. Changing a policy retimes the existing appearance; changing `appear_key` explicitly restarts it. Same-frame explicit render followed by a changed policy is re-evaluated rather than reusing stale motion.

Custom marks decide which datum coordinates to move. In a Plot marks callback, call `cui_plot_motion_follow` with the owner, a zero-initialized caller-owned pointer state, `options.motion.pointer`, the target coordinate and `hover->entering`. Use the returned coordinate when painting. This consumes Core time/reduced motion, requests redraw while moving and resets when the owner changes. Use independent states per coordinate/channel. The helper invalidates Core while moving; the host advances frames using `cui_needs_redraw`, including requests made by marks callbacks. It does not infer arbitrary host mark positions or replace the existing live-pointer/current-bounds host seam.

The low-level transition adapter borrows the explicitly supplied transition **only for synchronous stepping**. Do not retain an adapter. Opt-in Plot `on_appear` receives a sampler valid only during that callback. Published Plot state retains time/progress/flags and clears that temporary sampler's pointers. For later stagger sampling, use `cui_plot_motion_appear_stagger(&copied_policy.appear, &copied_state.appearance, ...)`; both values may safely be copied to other addresses. Legacy caller-supplied borrowed samplers retain their prior lifetime contract.

Validation rejects invalid enums, nonfinite/out-of-range timing and spring parameters before opt-in widget mutation. Factory, sample, stagger and follow failures preserve their outputs/state. Timing/delay is bounded to 3600 seconds in magnitude; response to 3600, damping to 100, epsilon to 65536. Negative delays are supported. Bezier overshoot is sampled as supplied; Chart reveal/hover and Plot hover opacity clamp to 0..1 for bounded rendering. Undamped springs may keep moving; positive damping settles. Appearance sampling does not rewrite caller data. Host data/callback/bitmap lifetimes remain unchanged.

The new native regression demonstrates two owners with different appearance/fade/pointer policies, actual Plot mark composition, line/bar/pie channel independence and settling, reversal, explicit instant/reduced overrides, legacy duration precedence, same-frame policy changes, invalid rollback and copied-state later sampling after original storage is overwritten. The standalone WASM browser probe paints real Chart bitmaps and Plot marks, drives actual mouse input and proves different reveal/pointer motion plus reduced completion. It owns fixed host bounds. No automatic current-frame prepaint/occlusion, OS long-press or native screen-reader parity is claimed.

## Integrator registration

Register `src/components/cui_plot_motion.c` in the styled library and web source lists; add `tests/unit/test_plot_chart_owner_motion.c` as a strict target linked to `cui`. In `scripts/amalgamate.py`, register `cui_plot_motion` with this component source and include it as an implementation dependency of Chart/PlotView and the aggregate Components header, alongside existing Plot and Theme dependencies. Regenerate distribution headers. Standalone probe `tests/web/test_plot_chart_owner_motion.py` builds its own scoped WASM fixture and requires pinned Zig 0.16.0/Playwright 1.58.0. No CMake/web/distribution registration was edited by this worker.

Validation on base `137e1c5`: strict native build passed without warnings; CTest 112/113 passed, with the sole distribution check reporting stale PlotView/Chart headers (root regeneration). The new behavior target was registered locally using an external CMake include. ASAN/UBSAN with leak detection and GCC fake-stack/use-after-return mode passed; Clang was unavailable. Standalone pinned Zig/WASM Chromium1208 canvas/mouse proof passed. Logs are `/home/matteo/.cache/cui-validation/owner-motion-next23-{handoff-build,commit-ctest,commit-asan,opacity-browser}.log`.

