# Select selection notifications

`cui_select_events` adds a synchronous post-mutation CHANGE observer to single-selection Styled Select. Existing `cui_select`, List, Menu, Combobox and multi-select APIs retain their behavior. Shared payloads come from [selection events](SELECTION_EVENTS.md); this is not uniform coverage of selection families or a bridge/binding typed-selection allowlist.

```c
#include <cui/cui_choice.h>

static void changed(void *user, const cui_selection_change *change) {
    cui_id *last_observed = user;
    *last_observed = change->value;
}

/* During active composition; owner keeps selected and popup between frames. */
static cui_select_event_result account_select(cui_context *ui, const cui_theme *theme,
    const cui_choice_item *items, size_t count, cui_id *selected,
    cui_choice_popup *popup, cui_id *last_observed) {
    return cui_select_events(ui, 2, theme, cui_string("Account"), cui_string("Choose"),
        items, count, selected, popup,
        (cui_choice_popup_options){.viewport = {0, 0, 640, 480}},
        (cui_selection_handler){changed, last_observed});
}
```

Initialize the popup once with `cui_choice_popup_init` and a stable nonzero popup ID. Existing item ID, popup state, viewport, theme and caller-array lifetime requirements apply. `cui_select_events` appends a `cui_selection_handler` to the existing Select arguments. It returns `cui_select_event_result`: `.legacy_changed` is the original final selection comparison, and `.events` contains preflight `valid`, active `submitted`, `change_count` of actual observer calls, and `count_overflow`. Missing observer preserves ordinary writes and reports zero. Counts saturate at `SIZE_MAX`; callbacks continue. Disabled controls may submit but emit no CHANGE; inert/layout-only ancestry emits none. Later rendering errors remain possible after successful preflight.

Select directly mutates `*selected`; this callback notifies an applied transition and cannot reject it. Every actual option-selection transition emits once, synchronously at its existing list reducer point. The payload copies previous and actual new selection, attempted `item`, ACTIVATION reason and common metadata. No label or borrowed query text is included. Zero/invalid selection remains caller-owned; filtering/reordering does not convert IDs to indices. Same-item activation can close the popup without CHANGE. Focus navigation/typeahead, trigger open/close, outside dismissal and external model assignments do not emit selection CHANGE.

The list processes its complete eligible input batch before Select closes its popup. A→B→A emits two notifications, while `.legacy_changed` remains false. Do not treat that bool as an event count or generate another callback from it. Observer owner writes affect later events and final painting/result in the existing pointer-owned model; they do not recursively synthesize notifications. Consuming later input changes its eligibility. Popup closing still occurs after the list pass, rather than immediately after the first callback.

`event.target` is the Select trigger ID. Genuine accepted activation evidence is copied only if Core already makes it available; `event.activation.target` then stays the actual child ID. The existing list normally registers option semantics after reduction, so HAS_ACTIVATION is absent at this seam. Preserve that registration timing. The fallback copies only actual frame/index and source proven by the triggering pointer-up, keyboard or accessibility event type. It does not invent a pointer position, touch snapshot, accessibility activation snapshot or an accepted activation merely from source. Presence flags, not zero-filled storage, establish evidence. See [shared metadata](COMPONENT_EVENTS.md).

Handler/user/payload are borrowed synchronously. Pointer-free payload copies survive later frames and context destruction. Keep active item arrays, popup/selected storage, users and rendering resources alive for their existing boundaries; changing copied options/handler affects a later call. Schedule removal/teardown for after submission/frame completion. Context destruction, freeing active state/resources and recursive same-frame submission during callbacks remain unsupported; no runtime destruction guard or new queue is added.

`tests/unit/test_select_events.c` uses real Select input to verify pointer/touch/key transitions, accessibility focus without selection and unsupported option activation, multiple changes returning to entry, same-item close, missing handler, owner updates, consumed later input, disabled/inert/omitted submission, legacy coexistence, copied payload lifetime and truthful evidence absence. Existing `test_choice.c` remains the regression suite for list callbacks, popup focus/close, scrolling and custom render slots. Native tests must use the private display wrapper:

```sh
python scripts/test_local.py --build-dir build -- -R '^(select_events|choice)$'
```

[Private plumbing checkpoint](design/SELECT_EVENTS_SLICE.md) explains the reducer seam. No new public List observer, Base Select, controlled selection request, confirmation/lifecycle event is implemented by this Select facade. [Combobox observers](COMBOBOX_EVENTS.md) have a separate ordering contract.

Accessibility boundary: current Core permits FOCUS for OPTION but its ACTIVATE role allowlist excludes OPTION. Select therefore supports accessibility focus followed by explicit keyboard navigation/selection, not a newly enabled accessibility option-activation path. Accessibility focus alone also leaves the retained list active cursor unchanged; the tested fallback explicitly navigates before Enter rather than claiming focused-option confirmation. Tests assert these boundaries without changing Core or claiming positive activation coverage.

documents.docs/SEPARATOR.md:
# Separator

`cui_separator_ex` composes a decorative horizontal or vertical one-pixel line. Use `cui_separator_defaults()` for a 160px horizontal solid line, then set the options for a dashed line, centered label or custom color.

```c
#include <cui/cui_components.h>

void draw_login_divider(cui_context *ui, const cui_theme *theme) {
    cui_separator_options options = cui_separator_defaults();
    options.length = 240;
    options.dashed = true;
    options.has_label = true;
    options.label = cui_string("Or continue with");
    cui_separator_ex(ui, cui_id_from(ui, "login-divider"), theme, options);
}
```

Dashes are 4px long with 2px gaps, starting at the beginning of the line and clipping the final dash to the explicit length. Set `vertical = true` to change the axis. The label sits at the center with the theme background behind it, muted text at 75% of the root font size, a root-font-sized line box, and horizontal/vertical padding of 8px/4px. `has_label` distinguishes no label from an explicitly empty label, which still paints the padded background. Labels and their background can extend past a short line.

The default line uses the theme border. Set both `custom_color = true` and `color` to override it; explicit alpha zero remains transparent. Keep label bytes valid through renderer completion. There is no retained state, callback or keyboard stop: submit the separator in each frame where it should appear.

Length zero selects 160px. Positive lengths up to 4096px are accepted. Negative/nonfinite/oversized lengths, missing label bytes, an invalid root font size or ID zero return false and submit nothing. Core retains its normal capacity-error behavior during valid submission. The length bound limits dashed geometry to at most 683 rectangles.

The implementation follows the axis, color, 4/2 dash and padded-label contracts in the [frozen Separator source](https://github.com/longbridge/gpui-kit/blob/87d10ae5e1299d1be18670a37570c5402f952e34/crates/component/src/separator.rs). CUI takes an explicit resolved length rather than automatically inheriting the parent's full width/height. Arbitrary GPUI style refinements and renderer-native dashed vector paths are not represented by this adapter; Core boxes provide explicit composition. The existing `cui_separator` preserves its earlier horizontal, unlabelled API and metrics.

Headless tests check both axes and line styles, dash positions, themed centered labels including empty labels, and rejection without frame output. Separator is a reusable primitive; it does not add a 79th entry to the pinned component manifest.

documents.docs/SETTINGS.md:
# Settings

Settings composes searchable pages, groups and editable fields with a resizable sidebar and a scrolling active page. It uses the existing Sidebar, Resizable, Switch, Checkbox, Input, NumberInput and Menu engines (with a legacy Select fallback). Custom callbacks can replace a field or an entire item, and receive the original page/group/item indices, size, layout and disabled state.

```c
bool notifications = true;
cui_setting_item items[] = {{
    .title = {"Notifications", 13}, .kind = CUI_SETTING_SWITCH,
    .boolean = &notifications, .has_default = true,
    .default_boolean = false
}};
cui_setting_group groups[] = {{
    .title = {"General", 7}, .items = items, .count = 1
}};
cui_setting_page pages[] = {{
    .title = {"Preferences", 11}, .icon = {"settings", 8},
    .groups = groups, .count = 1
}};
cui_settings_page_state workspace[1];
cui_settings_state state;
cui_settings_state_init(&state, workspace, 1, search_edit);
cui_settings_options options = cui_settings_default();
options.width = 800;
options.height = 500;
/* Inside a frame: */
cui_settings_result result = cui_settings(ui, id, &theme,
    pages, 1, &state, options);
```

The caller owns descriptors, field values, editor handles and all state. Keep these alive through rendering; callbacks execute synchronously while building the frame. Use separate state for each Settings instance. The search editor is optional and must be single-line. Without it, `options.query` supplies a controlled filter. Search text is reduced before navigation and content, so text queued in the current frame filters both immediately. Editors and popup state must be initialized using their own module APIs. Text fields borrow an Input state; numeric fields borrow a Number state and editor; source dropdown fields borrow an initialized `cui_settings_dropdown_state`, persistent Menu state and scratch arrays, described below. Existing `popup` descriptors keep the Select fallback. Optional Switch state enables its existing retained motion.

Filtering matches item titles, descriptions and keywords. Custom whole-item renderers match only keywords unless their `matches` hook supplies another policy. Page and group titles, group footers and title suffixes do not independently match. The default matcher folds ASCII case and preserves non-ASCII UTF-8 bytes; a host matching callback can provide Unicode lowercase matching. Filtered arrays retain original indices and IDs. A matching selected page/group remains selected; otherwise selection falls back to the first matching page and its page header. No-results filtering keeps selection but renders no stale page. Clearing search preserves the current selection.

The sidebar starts at 250 pixels and resizes within 160–360 by default. Page trees start collapsed unless `default_open` is set. Groups become sidebar entries only when their titles exist and the page has more than one matching group. Page activation selects its header; group activation selects the group and reveals it after measurement. Layout switches to stacked items when the content panel is at most 480 pixels wide. A vertical item override also stacks that item. Text fields use 256 pixels horizontally; NumberInput uses the source 128 pixels. Both grow when stacked. Zero width defaults to 800; negative width grows using the previous measured bounds. Zero height uses a CUI fallback of 500 rather than an unconstrained host viewport.

Medium is the default. Zero-initialized options select a page; set `default_group_selected` to select `default_group` initially. Switch, Checkbox, Input, NumberInput and dropdown triggers honor XS/S/M/L. Input and NumberInput frame heights are 20/24/32/44; the source Button trigger heights are 20/24/32/32. Text glyphs are centered inside their fixed line boxes. Settings forwards its size to custom callbacks. Source item typography uses 14-pixel labels, muted descriptions and centered 20-pixel Label line boxes (plain item descriptions use the inherited golden-ratio line height), scaled by `theme.font_size / 16`. Group surfaces support Normal, Fill and Outline, including a per-group override. Fill uses independently configurable `theme.group_box`; item titles use `theme.group_box_foreground`. Card padding is 16, item gap 16, title gap Normal16/card12 and footer gap 8 at that density. Footer callbacks receive a theme copy with small muted text defaults and render outside the surface. Disabled items paint at half opacity. Built-in controls receive disabled state; custom callbacks must honor `options.disabled` themselves.

Reset appears only when the active page permits reset and a matching item is dirty. The icon uses the named Undo2 SVG and the theme asset provider. The reset icon has a delayed hover/focus tooltip using `reset_label`, with state retained in each page workspace; Escape suppresses it until hover/focus leaves. Reset applies to every matching reset-capable item, including disabled items and unchanged matching defaults. `has_default` enables typed defaults; custom dirty/reset hooks take precedence. `on_change` runs after a built-in mutation or successful reset. Number fields keep out-of-range draft text while the setting value is clamped to the configured bounds; blur commits the clamped draft. `cui_settings_reset_page` exposes the same filtered reset policy without rendering. Text reset replaces the whole range atomically, so undo restores the original text and selection.

The state workspace capacity bounds page count to at most 4096. The current sidebar composition supports at most 256 groups per page; groups support at most 65,536 items, each with at most 4096 keywords. Invalid descriptors or nonfinite dimensions return `invalid`. Actual layout is also bounded by the core context capacity. Text edit errors are reported through `edit_error`. `cui_settings_part_id` provides deterministic IDs for headless routing; current navigation parts are page5/group6, field24, reset34 and search4. Resize and scroll controls use their owning modules' part-ID helpers.

## Checked Menu dropdown composition

Use caller-owned, per-field workspace for the source outline Button and checked Menu:

```c
cui_menu_entry menu_entries[3];
bool menu_checks[3];
cui_settings_dropdown_state menu_state;
cui_settings_dropdown_init(&menu_state, popup_id, menu_entries, menu_checks, 3);
/* On a dropdown item with three choices: */
item.dropdown_state = &menu_state;
```

All arrays and state remain alive across frames. Capacity must cover `choice_count`; missing arrays, an uninitialized popup or insufficient capacity returns `invalid` before rendering. Choice IDs remain globally unique Menu row IDs. The adapter reconstructs scratch descriptors and checks from the controlled `choice` each frame, reduces events once, then paints the selected label and ChevronDown SVG. The horizontal trigger fits its contents; a vertical trigger grows. The popup opens below the trigger aligned to its right edge, flips/clamps at the Settings viewport, uses source Medium 26-pixel Menu rows, skips disabled options during navigation and closes after activation. Selecting the current value closes without emitting a changed callback. External changes immediately update label and checks. Set `dropdown_scrollable` for the existing bounded wheel/touch-drag Menu window.

`cui_menu_popup_paint` supports this composition for other custom triggers. Call the drawing-free `cui_base_menu_tree` once, paint the trigger independently, then call the popup painter with its anchor. The painter does not process events, submit a trigger or mutate action/check values; it registers visible popup row semantics and retains layout/window metrics. Settings uses the shared paint-only Button renderer to avoid duplicate registration. For old descriptors with only `item.popup`, the initialized Choice popup still renders the legacy Select. This fallback retains its prior list appearance and is not the source checked Menu.

## Remaining source gaps

Oversized non-scrollable menus still use the bounded existing Menu window rather than the upstream unconstrained-height layout. The scrollable Menu window does not yet paint a source scrollbar. Tooltip overlay styling and slide/shared-tooltip grace motion remain those of the current Tooltip module. Numeric/string external settings are caller-controlled values/editors rather than retained getter/setter closures. String reset is one atomic range edit; undo restores both the original text and its pre-reset selection.

This implementation renders every matching group rather than the upstream virtualized List. Extremely large pages should be bounded by application and core capacities. Grow-width layout and group jumps depend on prior measured geometry and can require another frame; filtering resets page scrolling. Arbitrary style refinements, Markdown descriptions, translated default labels and custom Icon objects are represented by callbacks or explicit strings rather than source object APIs. Named/path icons use the theme provider. These limitations remain explicit; this module does not establish complete upstream parity.

documents.docs/SHADOW_PRESETS.md:
# Named shadow presets

`cui_shadow_preset_layers` copies the pinned public Styled DROP presets into caller storage. `cui_set_shadow_preset` applies the same list to the open box and replaces its entire mixed DROP/INSET list. `CUI_SHADOW_NONE` clears both kinds. A zero/default style has no implicit shadow.

```c
cui_begin_box(ui, card_id, card_box);
if (!cui_set_shadow_preset(ui, CUI_SHADOW_MD, card_radii)) {
    /* Handle rejected layout state or invalid radii. */
}
/* Card content. */
cui_end_box(ui);
```

The factory needs capacity for the selected list, at most `CUI_SHADOW_PRESET_MAX` (two). It writes the count and exactly the selected layer span; unused elements remain untouched. Unknown enums, insufficient capacity, capacity multiplication overflow, NULL required pointers, and count-output overlap with the written layer span reject before writing either output. NONE accepts a NULL layer buffer with capacity zero and writes only count zero. The caller supplies valid, suitably aligned storage. The factory retains no pointers and allocates no memory.

```c
cui_drop_shadow layers[CUI_SHADOW_PRESET_MAX];
size_t count;
if (cui_shadow_preset_layers(CUI_SHADOW_SM, layers, CUI_SHADOW_PRESET_MAX, &count)) {
    cui_box_shadow mixed[CUI_SHADOW_PRESET_MAX];
    for (size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i)
        mixed[i] = (cui_box_shadow){.layer = layers[i], .kind = CUI_BOX_SHADOW_DROP};
    cui_set_box_shadows(ui, mixed, count, card_radii);
}
```

Both existing setters copy their lists; caller stack storage may be reused after a successful setter. The render commands retain their existing value ownership and renderer lifetime contracts. NONE follows the existing zero-count setter: radii do not affect empty paint. An invalid preset leaves an already assigned list unchanged.

`cui_box_shadow_init(offset_x, offset_y, color, &out)` reproduces the explicit source constructor: DROP, zero sigma, zero spread, and the supplied offsets/color. It accepts finite offsets in [-4096,4096] and leaves output unchanged on rejection. Callers can then set bounded sigma/spread or choose INSET through the existing mixed-list API. This is an explicit constructor, not a default style layer.

| Preset | Ordered (offset_y, sigma, spread, black alpha byte) |
| --- | --- |
| NONE | empty |
| 2XS | (1, 0, 0, 13) |
| XS | (1, 2, 0, 13) |
| SM | (1, 3, 0, 26), (1, 2, -1, 26) |
| MD | (4, 6, -1, 26), (2, 4, -2, 26) |
| LG | (10, 15, -3, 26), (4, 6, -4, 26) |
| XL | (20, 25, -5, 26), (8, 10, -6, 26) |
| 2XL | (25, 50, -12, 64) |

Offsets on x are zero, RGB is black, all layers are DROP, and all distances are logical pixels. The sigma values are the source public macro values, without the component-private CSS blur halving. CUI rounds source Hsla alphas .05/.1/.25 to nearest 8-bit bytes 13/26/64. Canvas global-alpha composition has renderer-specific byte rounding; browser probes compare two-layer paint with independent opaque Canvas images and bound the one-layer difference from ideal arithmetic. Native software assertions retain exact quantized pixels. It therefore does not claim floating Hsla or native GPUI shader pixel identity. Ancestor opacity uses the existing Core command alpha conversion; interaction-disabled semantics alone do not remove the assigned decoration.

Source: pinned gpui-component0.7.1 `87d10ae5e1299d1be18670a37570c5402f952e34`, dependency `gpui-pre-macros0.3.8/src/styles.rs:384–490`; explicit constructor `gpui-pre0.3.8/src/style.rs:349–393`. Source fixture records the exact macro file hash. These are public named presets; private popover, toast, and raised-tab consumers are separate appearance work.

The focused native test checks all ordered source tuples, constructor bounds, output transactions, mixed-list replacement, software pixels below a translucent surface, ancestor opacity, and unchanged decoration under disabled semantics. The standalone browser fixture exercises all eight presets with the actual pure-C WASM raster and Canvas at DPR1/2, including literal one/two-layer composition and opacity. Existing renderer capability, budgets, mask culling, cache preparation, and DROP/INSET rendering contracts remain applicable. This convenience slice does not certify the full shadow family, native GPUI antialiasing/color identity, or the appearances of unrelated component owners.

documents.docs/TEXTAREA.md:
# Textarea

`cui_textarea` is a bounded multiline editor with measured soft wrapping, pointer selection, vertical keyboard navigation, selection highlights, caret painting and a scrolling viewport. It uses a caller-owned multiline `cui_edit` for text, selection and undo history. It is independent of the single-line Input renderer.

```c
char text[4096] = "First line\nSecond line";
cui_edit *edit = cui_edit_create((cui_edit_config){
    .buffer = text, .capacity = sizeof(text), .multiline = true,
    .history_bytes = 8192, .max_history_steps = 64
});
cui_textarea_line rows[256];
cui_textarea_state state;
cui_textarea_state_init(&state, rows, 256);
/* Each frame, inside an open layout box: */
cui_textarea_result result = cui_textarea(ui, id, &theme,
    cui_string("Description"), edit, &state,
    (cui_textarea_options){.width = 320, .rows = 4,
        .placeholder = cui_string("Write a description"), .clipboard = platform});
```

Keep the editor, row workspace and state alive across frames. Each widget needs its own state. Text buffers must remain valid and unchanged until the draw list has been rendered. Destroy the editor when finished. No Textarea allocation or hidden cache is used. Text is limited to 65,536 bytes and the row workspace to 65,536 entries. Invalid inputs return `CUI_TEXTAREA_INVALID`; insufficient measured-row capacity returns `CUI_TEXTAREA_CAPACITY` and suppresses partial painting. An edit may succeed before exhausting visual-row capacity; expand the workspace rather than treating this error as a text rollback. Text-buffer capacity errors are reported separately through `edit_error`.

The zero-initialized options use the source Medium metrics: font 14, line height 20, horizontal padding 10, vertical padding 8, one row, appearance and border enabled. Preset metrics scale by `theme.font_size / 16`. XS/S/M/L fonts are 12/14/14/16; horizontal padding is 4/8/10/12 and vertical padding 0/2/8/10. Custom size uses font `custom_size * .875` and horizontal/vertical padding 8/2 at the same density. Width zero selects the CUI fallback 280; a negative width grows. Positive height overrides row height. Auto-grow defaults to one through ten rows and can be bounded explicitly. It is an optional CUI policy, not the upstream initial mode.

Soft wrapping preserves every byte and prefers whitespace boundaries. LF, CRLF and CR split hard lines; a final newline creates an empty final row. A cursor at a soft boundary has upstream/downstream affinity, so Left/Right can move between the two visual positions without changing the byte offset. Up/Down and Page Up/Down preserve a measured horizontal target; Home/End operate on the visual row, and command Home/End on the whole buffer. Movement reveals the caret. Pointer dragging and Shift navigation extend selection. Clipboard copy/cut/paste use the supplied synchronous platform callbacks; read-only fields retain navigation, selection and copy. Disabled fields reject interaction. External `cui_edit` mutations are detected through revision and selection changes, without reporting them as edits performed by the widget.

Enter inserts a newline and reports `pressed_enter`, `enter_secondary` and `enter_shift`. With `submit_on_enter`, ordinary or secondary Enter reports submission without insertion; Shift-Enter still inserts. These fields describe the last handled Enter in a frame. Read-only and disabled fields do not report submission.

Editable Textarea consumes Tab and Shift-Tab for indentation by default, including repeated key events. `tab_size` defaults to two spaces; `hard_tabs` inserts one tab byte instead. A collapsed Tab inserts at the cursor. A selection indents every touched hard line, including a line where the selection ends at its start. Shift-Tab removes only an exact matching prefix at each line start. An unchanged outdent creates no history step. Each successful operation is one undo step that restores the original selection; redo restores the mapped endpoints. Capacity failure leaves text, history and selection unchanged. Set `no_indent` to use normal Tab focus traversal. Read-only and disabled fields also retain normal focus traversal.

The semantic node carries a Textbox role, multiline, read-only, required, invalid, disabled and selection/scroll details. `name` labels the semantic node; it is not a visible label. A custom role and removal from Tab stops are available. The theme's input background/border, text, muted, selection and independent caret tokens are consumed. Reduced motion gives a steady caret. Otherwise focused fields invalidate for caret blinking.

## Remaining source gaps

This is a scalar UTF-8 editor, not the upstream shaped editor. Grapheme-aware movement, word navigation, bidi shaping and cluster hit testing, IME preedit, inline atomic tokens, multiple cursors, language/editor features, rich paste interception and native context menus remain unsupported. Numeric Tab ordering and external accessibility IDs also require shared APIs. No parity claim is made for these behaviors.

Grow-width wrapping uses the previous tracked viewport, with a 280-pixel initial fallback; same-frame intrinsic width changes can therefore require another frame. Placeholder painting uses the core text renderer rather than an independently measured multiline placeholder workspace. There is no source focus shadow or independently configured line-height option. Explicitly sized fields avoid the initial grow-width measurement limitation.

Keyboard events marked `ime_composing` or `prefer_character_input` are left unconsumed by Input, the edit engine's routed event helper, and Textarea. They cannot navigate, edit, invoke clipboard shortcuts, submit or insert a newline. Committed `CUI_EVENT_TEXT` still inserts normally, and ordinary keyboard navigation resumes afterward. This protects host composition ownership; rendering preedit text and candidate windows remains a host integration gap.

Vertical and horizontal scroll offsets are applied to the clipped viewport, so virtual row culling and paint translation agree. Repeated Down at the final line keeps that line visible; Ctrl+Home returns to the first line. The caret model and the text drawing positions are tested together.

documents.docs/THEME_PRESETS.md:
# Named theme presets

CUI includes 38 named palette adaptations from the pinned GPUI Kit theme sources: 12 light and 26 dark. The source colors, author metadata, license and revision are retained in `third_party/gpui_themes/`.

```c
cui_theme theme;
size_t index = 0;
if (cui_theme_preset_get(index, &theme)) {
    cui_str name = cui_theme_preset_name(index);
    bool dark = cui_theme_preset_is_dark(index);
    /* Use theme when rendering components. */
    (void)name;
    (void)dark;
}
```

Enumerate indices below `cui_theme_preset_count()`. Invalid indices return an empty name and `false`; a failed `get` leaves the output untouched. Returned names have static lifetime. Presets initialize a complete theme, including default metrics and motion settings. Apply application overrides, including an SVG icon loader, after loading a preset.

System appearance is a host preference: select the light or dark default when it changes. Named presets are explicit choices and should remain selected across system appearance changes. Persist names rather than indices if preferences must survive catalog reordering.

These are adaptations to CUI's semantic color tokens, not a claim of complete GPUI theme rendering parity. Missing source tokens use derived colors; button interaction colors are derived where necessary. Font and syntax-highlighting themes are not included. Aurora's unsupported gradient brushes use their first color stop; the generated JSON records affected tokens in `adaptations`.

`python scripts/theme_presets.py` regenerates both the portable C table and website JSON from the same pinned palette data. `make lint` checks that these outputs remain synchronized.

documents.docs/TYPOGRAPHY.md:
# Typography controls

`cui_text_ex` combines a logical pixel font size, an optional logical line height,
a color, and `cui_text_style`. Styles offer Normal, Medium, Semibold, and Bold
weights, independent italic and monospace flags, and an explicit family ID.
Use `cui_text_line` for unwrapped text with the same controls. The basic
`cui_text` API uses the default family's regular face and its measured height.
Font size and line height are independent: use the selected face's actual font
metrics when alignment requires its ascent, descent, or leading.

For reusable application styles, use the shipped header-only
[`cui_typography_roles` and `cui_typography_token` helpers](TYPOGRAPHY_TOKENS.md).
They resolve caller-owned body, heading and code defaults into a copy that can be
overridden locally; they do not mutate the theme or a global current style:

```c
#include "cui/cui_typography.h"

cui_typography_token title;
if (cui_typography_resolve(&roles, CUI_TYPOGRAPHY_HEADING, &title)) {
    title.align = CUI_TEXT_ALIGN_CENTER;
    bool submitted = cui_typography_text(ui, cui_string("Workspace"), title, ink);
    (void)submitted; /* Handle failure and end-frame diagnostics in the host. */
}
```

Here `roles` and `ink` are application-owned values; call inside an active frame.
Resolution validates only the selected role and preserves output on failure.
See the token guide for a full role initializer, color spans, exact native-face
registration, and wide/narrow composed tests. Use `cui_typography_text_painted`
for [linear gradient glyph paint](TEXT_GRADIENT.md) on supported renderers.
Tracking, variable axes and arbitrary numeric weights remain future work.

Family IDs are application-defined `uint32_t` keys. Zero retains the default
family. Register regular and variant faces for a named family on the same
`cui_font` used by the context's measurement callbacks and renderer:

```c
const uint32_t editorial = 7;
cui_text_style body = {.family = editorial};
cui_text_style title = {.family = editorial, .weight = CUI_TEXT_WEIGHT_BOLD};
/* Check registration results and supply application-owned font paths. */
bool ready = cui_font_register_style(font, body, body_path) &&
             cui_font_register_style(font, title, bold_path);
if (ready) {
    cui_text_metrics_changed(ui); /* Between frames, before the next layout. */
    /* Inside the next frame: */
    cui_text_ex(ui, cui_string("Editorial heading"), 30, ink, 36, title);
}
```

Registration is per call, so a successful regular registration remains if the
following bold registration fails. For an atomic application font switch, prepare
a separate font object and its registrations, then update the wrapper host
and renderer references together between frames and invalidate affected contexts.
If the context uses the font directly as its fixed `measure_user`, recreate that
context to bind another font, or change registrations on the existing font.
Keep the old font alive until its outstanding draw lists have been consumed. Fonts and paths are explicit resources; these APIs do not
fetch fonts, discover installed families, or synthesize bold or italic faces.
An unset named-family variant uses that family's regular face. Unknown families
fall back to the default family's requested style and then its regular face.

The native `cui_font_measure`, `cui_font_measure_styled`, and
`cui_font_get_metrics` functions already accept `void *` as their first
parameter and match the corresponding `cui_config` callback types. Direct
assignment is valid when `measure_user` is the font itself. No function-pointer
cast is needed. If your user pointer contains application state instead, use
wrappers that extract its font, as in this complete C11 program:

```c
#include "cui/cui_text.h"

typedef struct {
    cui_font *font;
} text_host;

static cui_size text_measure(void *user, cui_str text, float size) {
    text_host *host = user;
    return cui_font_measure(host->font, text, size);
}
static cui_size text_measure_styled(void *user, cui_str text, float size,
                                    cui_text_style style) {
    text_host *host = user;
    return cui_font_measure_styled(host->font, text, size, style);
}
static bool text_metrics(void *user, float size, cui_text_style style,
                         cui_font_metrics *out) {
    text_host *host = user;
    return cui_font_get_metrics(host->font, size, style, out);
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    /* Paths: default regular face, editorial regular face, editorial bold face. */
    if (argc != 4)
        return 1;
    text_host host = {.font = cui_font_open(argv[1])};
    if (!host.font)
        return 1;
    cui_text_style body = {.family = 7};
    cui_text_style title = {.family = 7, .weight = CUI_TEXT_WEIGHT_BOLD};
    if (!cui_font_register_style(host.font, body, argv[2]) ||
        !cui_font_register_style(host.font, title, argv[3])) {
        cui_font_close(host.font);
        return 1;
    }
    cui_context *ui = cui_create((cui_config){
        .measure = text_measure,
        .measure_styled = text_measure_styled,
        .font_metrics = text_metrics,
        .measure_user = &host
    });
    if (!ui) {
        cui_font_close(host.font);
        return 1;
    }
    if (!cui_begin_frame(ui, 600, 300, .016f)) {
        cui_destroy(ui);
        cui_font_close(host.font);
        return 1;
    }
    cui_text_ex(ui, cui_string("Editorial heading"), 30,
                (cui_color){20, 20, 20, 255}, 36, title);
    cui_text_ex(ui, cui_string("Body copy"), 16,
                (cui_color){20, 20, 20, 255}, 24, body);
    cui_draw_list draws = cui_end_frame(ui);
    int status = draws.count && cui_last_error(ui) == CUI_OK ? 0 : 1;
    /* A real host paints draws with host.font before ending its lifetime. */
    cui_destroy(ui);
    cui_font_close(host.font);
    return status;
}
```

Save the complete program as `typography.c`. With the default native library
build and existing FreeType/HarfBuzz development packages, compile it with:

```sh
cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror -Iinclude typography.c build/libcui_text.a build/libcui.a $(pkg-config --cflags --libs freetype2 harfbuzz) -lm -o typography
./typography /path/default-regular.ttf /path/editorial-regular.ttf /path/editorial-bold.ttf
```

Link the optional native `cui_text` adapter and Core; its FreeType/HarfBuzz
requirements are described in [TEXT.md](TEXT.md). This example submits a draw
list; the application supplies its renderer and window/event loop. The host
struct and font must outlive the context's measurement callbacks and every draw
that uses them. A custom renderer maps the complete style, including family,
to the same faces used for measurement. Reuse stable family IDs, and call
`cui_text_metrics_changed` between frames in every affected context after
changing registrations.

See [TEXT.md](TEXT.md) for ownership, fallback ordering, native registration,
thread confinement, and headless test fixture details. These controls are text
primitives; they do not imply that every component has a per-control family
option or that TextView supports arbitrary family changes within a shaped run.

